\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 New York Passes Bill to Let Doctors Mail  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills to States With Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Emily Bazelon \par \uc2 1,603 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The measure is one of several recently enacted by states aiming to expand access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for patients who live where it is outlawed.\par \par \uc2 The New York State Legislature gave final approval on Tuesday to legislation that provides legal protection for New York doctors to prescribe and send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients in states that have outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The measure, along with similar new laws in several other states controlled by Democrats, could significantly expand medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access by allowing more patients in states that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to end pregnancies at home, without traveling to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 The New York bill now goes to the desk of Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who has indicated that she supports the idea of such a shield law. The bill stipulates that New York courts and officials will not cooperate if a state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban tries to prosecute, sue or otherwise penalize a New York health care provider who offers \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  via telemedicine to a patient in that state, as long as the provider complies with New York law. It passed the State Assembly by a vote of 99 to 45 on Tuesday evening after clearing the Senate by a vote of 39 to 22 last month.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court ended the nationwide right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year, legislation known as telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield laws have been enacted in Massachusetts, Colorado, Vermont and Washington. But New York's legislation is expected to have a notable impact. Several providers in New York say they plan to send \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to patients in all restrictive states, and a few providers are speaking publicly, which those in other states with shield laws have so far not done.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm going to mail pills as soon as the governor signs the bill,'' said Dr. Linda Prine, a New York physician and co-founder of the Miscarriage and \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Hotline, which answers patients' questions about medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''This is the first time we're able to do something to fight back,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Prine said she and at least three other doctors would mail pills to patients in all states with restrictions or bans. A telemedicine service in New York, Juniper Midwifery, also said it hoped to use the shield law to mail pills to patients in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 ''It's definitely something that we would want to explore and make it happen,'' Jillian Barovick, a midwife and co-founder of Juniper, said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now mostly outlawed in 14 states. But most bans penalize people who assist with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not those who take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 More than half of abortions in the United States are now carried out with medication, using two drugs, mifepristone followed by misoprostol. Data indicates the process is safe and effective.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents are trying to force mifepristone off the market by challenging the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of the drug 23 years ago in a lawsuit expected to be decided by the Supreme Court. The suit also challenges the F.D.A.'s decision in 2021 allowing patients to receive prescribed pills by mail.\par \par \uc2 Since the overturning of Roe v. Wade, tens of thousands of patients in states with bans or severe restrictions have taken \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. Many have traveled to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal to obtain pills at clinics or at addresses in those states where pills were sent by mail. But many patients cannot afford the cost and time of traveling.\par \par \uc2 ''The telemedicine option and protecting providers who are providing telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services is going to help tremendously the people here in Mississippi and other restricted states,'' said Michelle Col\u243\'00\'F3n, the executive director of SHERo Mississippi, an organization focused on supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  for people of color. ''This will expand access, which we so desperately need.''\par \par \uc2 Other patients receive pills in the mail from overseas, either prescribed by doctors abroad, through a European telemedicine service, or ordered directly from online pharmacies in India or other countries. By the time the pills arrive, often two to three weeks later, patients may be past the 12-week threshold of pregnancy that the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  endorses for using medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mailing pills from within the United States, as doctors operating under shield laws are doing, cuts delivery time to a few days.\par \par \uc2 The telemedicine shield laws are not a guarantee of full legal protection for providers. Under the legislation, if a state with a ban issued an arrest warrant for a physician like Dr. Prine, New York would refuse to extradite her to that state. But if she traveled there, or to another state that agreed to extradite her, she could face criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 ''Texas could prosecute them for murder,'' said Jonathan Mitchell, a former Texas solicitor general and the architect of a 2021 Texas law that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks into pregnancy and deputized private citizens to enforce it by suing for cash judgments of $10,000 per \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Under Texas law, killing a fetus through an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is no different from killing a baby, except that the mother cannot be prosecuted (or sued) for death of a fetus,'' Mr. Mitchell wrote in an email.\par \par \uc2 Because of the substantial risks, only about 10 health care providers in states with shield laws are known to have begun sending pills to patients in states that restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These providers have proceeded cautiously.\par \par \uc2 ''Most of the people who have been using the shield laws so far have been just dipping their toes in the water, going to states that maybe aren't quite as litigious, but I think we just need to start doing it to all of them,'' said a doctor in New York's Hudson Valley who is planning to send pills under the new shield legislation and spoke on the condition of anonymity because of the risk involved.\par \par \uc2 Other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have indicated interest in using shield laws if they could send prescriptions to a pharmacy that would mail the pills rather than stocking and shipping the medications themselves. That option could become available in the coming months if California enacts a telehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield bill that passed the state Senate last month. An online pharmacy based in California, Honeybee Health, hopes to use that law to ship to all 50 states, said Jessica Nouhavandi, co-founder and president of Honeybee.\par \par \uc2 ''We are anxiously awaiting the passing of the California shield law,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 As providers test their states' telehealth shield laws, many legal questions could arise, including the possibility of civil suits and challenges to a provider's medical license for unauthorized practice of medicine.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not a matter of if a provider will be challenged in court,'' said Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, the dean of Temple University Law School, who has written in support of shield laws. ''It's a matter of when.''\par \par \uc2 Lawyers on each side of the issue say that state shield laws undermine basic premises of interstate cooperation. Rather than recognizing one state's arrest warrant or court order, another state effectively throws a wrench into the enforcement of that state's laws.\par \par \uc2 And a law that shields telehealth abortions disrupts the usual model for telemedicine law and policy, which ''presumes that you're providing care in the place where the patient is,'' Professor Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9 said.\par \par \uc2 To treat a patient in Texas, for example, a New York physician would normally first get a Texas medical license. If the doctor practiced without one, New York would generally help Texas initiate disciplinary proceedings through its medical board. Without the shield law, an out-of-state ruling could have consequences for the doctor's license in New York.\par \par \uc2 In addition, in some cases, the U.S. Constitution requires states to enforce the civil judgments of another state. An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in New York could be sued in Texas, for example, by a family member of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient, claiming damages for wrongful death of the embryo or fetus. If the plaintiff won, the Full Faith and Credit Clause in the Constitution could obligate New York to comply in collecting damages.\par \par \uc2 ''It's very clear that in other states, citizens can still sue doctors who engage in the unlawful practice of medicine,'' said Denise Harle, senior counsel for the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , a conservative Christian legal organization that represents the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plaintiffs seeking to remove mifepristone from the market in their lawsuit against the F.D.A. ''They can sue to protect against bad acts by people who are committing crimes.''\par \par \uc2 Malpractice insurance can also be challenging to arrange. New York's shield bill, however, says that an insurer cannot drop or penalize a provider who complies with New York law in providing abortions.\par \par \uc2 To date, there have been no known examples of prosecutions, lawsuits or other actions against providers working under telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  shield laws. Dr. Prine and others said that they were prepared to be the guinea pigs but that they were also taking steps to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 Most providers using shield laws say they are avoiding travel to states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Dr. Prine said she would even make sure not to have a flight connection in an airport in a restrictive state.\par \par \uc2 Because of criminal risks, only about 10 health care providers in states with shield laws are known to have begun sending pills. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KELLY BURGESS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230621ej6l00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
