\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Split Iowa Supreme Court Blocks 6-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen \par \uc2 695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 A legal attempt to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Iowa after six weeks of pregnancy failed on Friday, after the State Supreme Court deadlocked over whether to vacate a lower court's injunction and allow the ban to take effect. That means \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain legal in Iowa through 20 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters\par \par \uc2 As one state after another banned or sharply limited access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Roe v. Wade was overturned, Iowa's status as a place where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained accessible was at stake.\par \par \uc2 The Iowa ban ''was dangerous, cruel and unconstitutional when the district court blocked it four years ago, and it's still dangerous, cruel and unconstitutional today,'' said Rita Bettis Austen, legal director of A.C.L.U. of Iowa, a civil liberties advocacy group. ''Many Iowans were depending on the outcome of the case today, and we are celebrating the preservation of our freedom, health and safety.''\par \par \uc2 Chris Schandevel, senior counsel for \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , which represented Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  of Iowa in the case, said, ''Iowans will surely be disappointed by today's result, and rightly so.'' He added, ''It's time for the Legislature to act -- once again -- to protect life.''\par \par \uc2 Background\par \par \uc2 The case revolved around a law signed in 2018 by Ms. Reynolds, a Republican, four years before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was codified in the Roe v. Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 Iowa's 2018 law was blocked by a district judge, who cited an Iowa Supreme Court decision holding that the State Constitution provided a fundamental right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a decision that was later reversed.\par \par \uc2 In light of that and the end of Roe, Ms. Reynolds asked the district court to lift its permanent injunction against enforcing the 2018 law. But the district court said no, setting the stage for the Iowa Supreme Court to hear the case.\par \par \uc2 The higher court, whose members are all Republican appointees, deadlocked 3-3 on Friday, letting the lower court's injunction stand.\par \par \uc2 In a sharply worded opinion, the three justices who opposed lifting the injunction called the state's request ''a poor vehicle'' to overrule the district court, writing, ''Nothing like this case has come up in Iowa's legal history before or is likely to come up again.''\par \par \uc2 What's Next in the Nation\par \par \uc2 State constitutions have become key arbiters in the nation's state-by-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles. In the states that have enacted bans, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights lawyers have argued that the bans are unconstitutional under state guarantees of privacy, health, liberty or family planning.\par \par \uc2 The state Supreme Courts that have already ruled in 2023 have split on the issue: South Carolina's ruled that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was included in its constitutional protections; North Dakota's and Oklahoma's found that their state Constitutions protected the right in some situations; Idaho's ruled that its Constitution did not protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As of June 9, 40 cases have been filed challenging \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in 22 states; 29 remain pending at either the trial or appellate levels, including cases in Indiana, Georgia and Florida, according to the Brennan Center for Justice.\par \par \uc2 What's Next in Iowa\par \par \uc2 The deadlocked vote means that this case is over, but no precedent has been set. The governor could call a special session of the Legislature to enact a new law -- as happened last month in South Carolina -- or push for a state constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks after oral arguments were presented in the case in April, Ms. Reynolds underscored her determination to continue pushing to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''The overturning of Roe v. Wade last year was just the beginning, and since then we've seen some in our movement concede ground to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists,'' the governor said in a recorded message to the Iowa Faith and Freedom Coalition. ''Not in Iowa, and not on my watch.''\par \par \uc2  rights supporters and anti-\uc2  activists outside a clinic in Iowa City last year. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOSEPH CRESS/IOWA CITY PRESS-CITIZEN, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230617ej6h0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
