\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Sentencing for Pills-by-Mail  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Opens a Heated Debate in Britain\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma Bubola \par \uc2 1,206 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The case has prompted heated debate in England, with some arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills should not be available by mail and others maintaining that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be fully decriminalized.\par \par \uc2 A woman's sentencing to prison this week for illegally using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to end a 32- to 34-week pregnancy is prompting a debate in England over the state of its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws and whether a woman should ever be prosecuted for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the debate is the fact that she was prosecuted under a law more than 160 years old. Some say that the law is too draconian, while others say the case illustrates the dangers of allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be sent by mail.\par \par \uc2 What happened in the case?\par \par \uc2 On Monday, a court in Stoke-on-Trent, a city in central England, sentenced Carla Foster, 44, to 28 months for having caused her own miscarriage by taking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills when she was in her eighth month of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The sentence includes up to 14 months in prison, after which she could serve the rest of her term on release if she meets certain conditions.\par \par \uc2 In early 2020, during the coronavirus pandemic, Ms. Foster, a mother of three, had moved back in with her long-term but estranged partner after becoming pregnant by another man, according to court documents.\par \par \uc2 The judge, Edward Brian Pepperall, wrote in his sentencing remarks that Ms. Foster had been in emotional turmoil as she sought to hide the pregnancy. She also repeatedly searched online for information about how to terminate her pregnancy in the first months of 2020, according to the judge.\par \par \uc2 That May, she obtained \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs by mail after giving false information to Britain's pregnancy advisory service, the judge wrote. Although \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are available by mail through the service in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy, Ms. Foster's internet searches indicated that she knew she was more than 24 weeks pregnant, the legal limit for most abortions, the court documents said.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after she took the drugs, her pregnancy ended in a stillbirth, according to the court. Paramedics were on the scene, and Ms. Foster told them that she understood that she would be required to speak to the police.\par \par \uc2 A post-mortem examination confirmed that the pregnancy was between 32 and 34 weeks, the judge wrote. A full-term pregnancy is about 40 weeks, or nine months.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Foster initially pleaded not guilty, but this March she pleaded guilty to a charge of ''administering poison with intent to procure a miscarriage.''\par \par \uc2 The judge wrote in his decision that Ms. Foster was a good mother to her three children, including one with special needs, and he acknowledged that they would suffer from their mother's imprisonment.\par \par \uc2 In handing down the sentence, the judge wrote, ''The balance struck by the law between a woman's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the rights of her unborn fetus is an emotive and often controversial issue.''\par \par \uc2 What are England's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws?\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been legal in England, Scotland and Wales since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act of 1967, and access to the procedure is generally liberal, experts said.\par \par \uc2 Abortions are allowed in the first 24 weeks of pregnancy and must be approved by two doctors.\par \par \uc2 In the first 10 weeks, women can get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by having two drugs prescribed to them, which in earlier years would have typically required a visit to a clinic. During the pandemic, when securing in-person services became both difficult and dangerous, the \uc2 British government\uc2  ruled that the drugs could be provided without an in-person appointment.\par \par \uc2 That decision was made permanent last August.\par \par \uc2 Later-term abortions are allowed in some exceptional cases, including when the woman's health is in danger or in some cases of fetal abnormality.\par \par \uc2 Yet when Parliament passed the 1967 legislation allowing for abortions, it did not repeal an earlier law that had criminalized them. In rare cases, therefore, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can still be prosecuted as a criminal act.\par \par \uc2 Under a law passed in 1861, any woman who takes ''poison'' with an intent to cause her own fetus's miscarriage ''shall be guilty of felony'' and liable ''to be kept in penal servitude for life.''\par \par \uc2 That was the law under which Ms. Foster was sentenced, and hers is not an isolated case.\par \par \uc2 In his ruling, Judge Pepperall cited a 2013 decision in which a British court sentenced a woman to three and a half years in prison for causing her own miscarriage while about 38 weeks pregnant. And \uc2 Stella Creasy\uc2 , a lawmaker in the opposition Labour Party, said on BBC Two's ''Newsnight'' current-affairs show on Monday that there had been 67 investigations under the 1861 act in the past decade.\par \par \uc2 What were the calls for decriminalization?\par \par \uc2 Caroline Nokes\uc2 , a lawmaker in the governing Conservative Party who chairs the Women and Equalities Committee in the House of Commons, told the BBC after the court's ruling that England was ''relying on legislation that is very out of date.''\par \par \uc2 She said the sentencing ''makes a case for Parliament to start looking at this issue in detail.''\par \par \uc2 Louise McCudden, the head of external affairs at MSI Reproductive Choices, a Britain-based women's health organization, said in an interview that even if prosecution was rare, the law must be changed.\par \par \uc2 ''Any case is too many,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Creasy, the Labour lawmaker, told BBC Two's ''Newsnight'' that ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a health care issue, not a criminal matter.''\par \par \uc2 What were the calls for tighter rules?\par \par \uc2 Groups that campaign against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  argued that the case illustrated the tragic consequences of the government's decision to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be sent by mail. They also said that Parliament should act based on the case, by further restricting the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''The government should urgently turn back the clock and end this disastrous policy of cheap, convenient, pills-by-post abortions,'' Andrea Williams, the chief executive of Christian Concern, an advocacy group, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 ''At 32 weeks' gestation, an unborn baby has a 95 percent chance of survival if delivered,'' she added. ''Our \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law rightly recognizes that these precious humans deserve protection.''\par \par \uc2 James Mumford, an author who studies political theology, modern Catholic social thought, and bioethics, told BBC Two's ''Newsnight'' on Monday that England had an ''extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -on-demand culture.''\par \par \uc2 He called Ms. Foster's case a natural consequence of making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills available by mail, which he called an ''utter disaster.''\par \par \uc2 What did medical experts say?\par \par \uc2 Some medical professionals also lobbied against sending Ms. Foster to prison, a penalty that they said might discourage other women from seeking access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at home.\par \par \uc2 The court said it had received a letter from the president of the Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists as well as other medical bodies urging a noncustodial sentence.\par \par \uc2 The judge responded that it was inappropriate for medical professionals to send such a letter, and that if they disagreed with the law, they should lobby Parliament to change it rather than judges who are charged with applying it.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A9. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230614ej6e0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
