\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Top Oklahoma Court Blocks 2 Laws Restricting  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacey Fortin \par \uc2 603 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 June 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma's Supreme Court said on Wednesday that two laws passed last year that ban most abortions are unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 But the ruling does not affect a law passed in 1910 which still prohibits most abortions in the state, unless they are necessary to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The laws that were struck down by the court were civil laws that had relied on suits from private citizens to enforce them. Both had made exceptions for cases involving a ''medical emergency.''\par \par \uc2 But the justices took issue with that language in their 6-3 ruling, which suggested that the exceptions were too narrow. They maintained that a woman has a constitutional right to end a pregnancy in order to save her life, without specifying the need for a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma is among a number of Republican-led states that moved to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in anticipation of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade last year. Legal challenges were quick to follow, and many cases ended up before state supreme courts. Those courts have become critical arbiters in deciding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and a new political front in the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles. In some conservative states, courts have decided that their state constitutions protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The decision also highlighted legal complications around how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and exceptions might be interpreted in cases where a woman's life is at risk. Doctors in other states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans said they have struggled to provide care for patients without breaking the law.\par \par \uc2 ''We hope that this decision provides some clarity, and that doctors can move forward,'' said Rabia Muqaddam of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who was the lead lawyer for the groups challenging the law. ''Now they can provide care to patients who are facing difficult health circumstances, and they can rely on their medical judgment.''\par \par \uc2 Background\par \par \uc2 Challenges to Oklahoma's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws were filed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organizations including Oklahoma Call for Reproductive Justice, a nonprofit volunteer organization based in Oklahoma City, and medical practitioners including the Tulsa Women's Reproductive Clinic.\par \par \uc2 The laws that were struck down on Wednesday had been modeled on legislation that took effect in Texas in 2021, which banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, and relied on civilian instead of criminal enforcement to work around court challenges.\par \par \uc2 Another law passed in Oklahoma last year, which had made it a felony to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''except to save the life of a pregnant woman in a medical emergency,'' was struck down by the state's Supreme Court in March. In their opinion on Wednesday, the justices cited their March decision, which was similarly concerned with the potential for strict interpretations of the term ''medical emergency.''\par \par \uc2 What's Next\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still largely illegal in Oklahoma because of the 1910 law that came back into effect when Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 ''Except for certain circumstances outlined in that statute, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still unlawful in the State of Oklahoma,'' Gentner Drummond, the state's attorney general, said in a statement on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 ''This ruling, while providing clarity in emergency situations, does not change the landscape of care significantly,'' said Emily Wales, the president of Planned Parenthood Great Plains, which was among the organizations that challenged the laws.\par \par \uc2 A rally in early May at the State Capitol in Oklahoma City. A 1910 law means \uc2  remains largely illegal in the state. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SUE OGROCKI/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230601ej610003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
