\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Judge Halts  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban In S. Carolina\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani \par \uc2 496 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 A South Carolina judge on Friday temporarily blocked a new law restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The order came just one day after Gov. \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 , a Republican, signed the six-week ban into law, and temporarily restores \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state to up to 22 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Greenville Women's Clinic -- an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in South Carolina -- and Planned Parenthood South Atlantic sued the state shortly after Mr. McMaster signed the law.\par \par \uc2 ''The status quo should be maintained'' until the Supreme Court can weigh in, said Judge Clifton Newman in his order blocking the new ban. ''It's going to end up there.''\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters\par \par \uc2 South Carolina has become an important access point for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the South as other states in the region have banned the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''Our doors remain open, and we are here to provide compassionate and judgment-free health care to all South Carolinians,'' said Jenny Black, president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood South Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Background\par \par \uc2 South Carolina legislators struggled for months to come to an agreement on an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban after the Supreme Court last year overturned Roe v. Wade and eliminated the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Republicans fought over how far a ban should go, and what exceptions to allow.\par \par \uc2 Three Republican women were part of a group of legislators who had tried to block a near-total ban.\par \par \uc2 The ban that finally passed prohibits most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy and requires any woman seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to first have two in-person doctor's visits and two ultrasounds.\par \par \uc2 The law allows exceptions for victims of rape and incest, and in cases of fatal fetal abnormalities or where the woman's life and health are at risk, but those exceptions are only available up to 12 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The ban is similar to an earlier six-week ban overturned by the State Supreme Court last year, known as a heartbeat bill, because cardiac activity can be detected around that time.\par \par \uc2 The court ruled that the South Carolina Constitution provides a right to privacy that includes the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''While I respect Judge Newman's decision, I remain convinced that the heartbeat bill is constitutional and that the Supreme Court will agree,'' said the Senate's president, Thomas Alexander, a Republican.\par \par \uc2 What's Next\par \par \uc2 The case now heads to the State Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Republican legislators said they made changes in the new law to address the court's objections to the previous law.\par \par \uc2 There has also been a change to the court that some feel might work in their favor: The justice who wrote the January decision was the only woman on the court. She has since retired and was replaced by a man, making South Carolina the only state with an all-male high court.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230527ej5r0003o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
