\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Indiana Reprimands Doctor Who Provided  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  to 10-Year-Old\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani \par \uc2 945 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dr. Caitlin Bernard violated the privacy of her young patient by discussing the girl's case with a reporter, the state's medical board ruled.\par \par \uc2 An Indiana doctor who provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 10-year-old rape victim last year violated her young patient's privacy by discussing the case with a reporter, the state's medical board ruled Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Caitlin Bernard, an Indianapolis obstetrician-gynecologist, catapulted into the national spotlight last year after she provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for an Ohio girl soon after the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, which left states free to severely restrict or outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The state's medical board voted to issue Dr. Bernard a letter of reprimand and a fine of $3,000. But it decided against stiffer penalties, which could have included suspension or probation, instead deciding that Dr. Bernard is fit to return to her practice.\par \par \uc2 The board also cleared her of other allegations that she failed to appropriately report the girl's rape to authorities.\par \par \uc2 The decision was the culmination of a yearlong legal pursuit of Dr. Bernard by the state's attorney general, Todd Rokita, a Republican who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Ohio girl had traveled to Indiana for the procedure after her home state enacted a ban on most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. Dr. Bernard told a reporter for The Indianapolis Star about the case during an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights rally. She didn't name the patient, but the case quickly became a flash point in the early, heated days of debate after the Supreme Court ruling, catching the attention of President Biden and turning conservative attention and ire toward Dr. Bernard.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think she intended for this to go viral,'' said Dr. John Strobel, the president of the board, calling Dr. Bernard a ''good doctor.''\par \par \uc2 ''But I do think we as physicians need to be more careful in this situation,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rokita, who had filed the complaints against Dr. Bernard with the medical board, praised the outcome.\par \par \uc2 ''This case was about patient privacy and the trust between the doctor and the patient that was broken,'' Mr. Rokita said in a statement late Thursday. ''What if it was your child or your patient or your sibling who was going through a sensitive medical crisis, and the doctor, who you thought was on your side, ran to the press for political reasons?''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard has criticized Mr. Rokita for turning the case into a ''political stunt.''\par \par \uc2 During the hearing, which stretched for more than 15 hours, ending just before midnight, Dr. Bernard said that her own comments did not reveal the patient's protected health information. Rather, Dr. Bernard said, it was the fierce political battle that followed. Some conservatives doubted her story and drove a demand to confirm it. Eventually, the man accused of raping the girl appeared in court and was linked to her case.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard, who has publicly advocated for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said she had an ethical obligation to educate the public about urgent matters of public health, especially questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- her area of expertise.\par \par \uc2 Last July, after Indiana scheduled a special legislative session on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Dr. Bernard was concerned that lawmakers in her home state would pass strict restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access similar to the Ohio law that forced her 10-year-old patient to cross state lines.\par \par \uc2 Indiana passed legislation banning most abortions, with narrow exceptions for rape and incest. That law is on hold pending a legal challenge. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently legal in Indiana up to 22 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard said she wanted to highlight the potential consequences of laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and ''did not anticipate'' how much the public would focus on the Ohio girl's case.\par \par \uc2 ''I think its incredibly important for people to understand the real-word impacts of the laws of this country,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Peter Schwartz, a Pennsylvania OB-GYN and chair of the American Medical Association's Council on Ethical and Judicial Affairs, supported Dr. Bernard's decision to speak out about the Ohio patient.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Schwartz said Dr. Bernard had an ''affirmative obligation to speak out'' about issues of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting that she is one of just two doctors in Indiana with expertise in complicated obstetric cases like second-trimester abortions.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys on both sides of the hearing called experts on medical confidentiality to understand if Dr. Bernard violated guidelines of the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, known as HIPAA, which governs the protection of patient privacy.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Bernard's employer, \uc2 Indiana University Health\uc2 , found that she did not violate HIPAA rules because the patient was not identifiable based on the information that Dr. Bernard had shared publicly.\par \par \uc2 ''The cause and effect that happened here was not: 'Dr. Bernard's story leads to the patient having her protected information shared,''' said Alice Morical, the doctor's attorney.\par \par \uc2 But members of the medical board, made up of six doctors and one attorney -- all appointed by the governor -- decided that, taken together, the details Dr. Bernard provided about the patient -- including her age, her rape, her home state and her \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- qualified as identifying information.\par \par \uc2 ''Dr. Bernard is a skilled and competent doctor, and I would submit that she is exactly the doctor that people would want their children to see under these circumstances,'' said Ms. Morical.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Caitlin Bernard, an obstetrician-gynecologist who provided an \uc2  for a young rape victim. (PHOTOGRAPH BY AMANDA ANDRADE-RHOADES FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230527ej5r0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
