\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 South Carolina Senate Passes 6-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban After Filibuster by 5 Women Fails\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike and Ava Sasani \par \uc2 1,142 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The legislation, which faced a filibuster led by five women senators, now heads to the governor who has said he will sign it.\par \par \uc2 The South Carolina Senate passed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy on Tuesday, after a filibuster led by five women senators, including three Republicans, failed to block it. The bill will drastically reduce access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a state that has become an unexpected destination for women seeking the procedure as almost every other Southern state has moved toward bans.\par \par \uc2 The legislation now heads to Gov. \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2 , a Republican who has said he will sign it. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates said they would challenge the ban in court, where it would test a State Supreme Court ruling in January that struck down a previous six-week ban and found a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The legislation had exposed divisions among Republicans over how far to go in restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a struggle that has played out in other legislatures in the year since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade, returning the regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states.\par \par \uc2 At least 25 states have moved to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the overturning of Roe last June. Fourteen of those states now ban most abortions. South Carolina is poised to join Georgia in prohibiting the procedure after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 The women who filibustered, calling themselves the ''Sister Senators,'' argued that the South Carolina bill set up so many hurdles that almost no one would be able to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state. Because pregnancy is considered to start on the first day of a woman's last menstrual period, six weeks is roughly two weeks after she has missed a period, before many women know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The bill requires any woman seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to first have two in-person doctors' visits and two ultrasounds. Senator Katrina Shealy, one of the Republican women who opposed the six-week ban, said on Tuesday: ''We are not God. We need to let people make decisions for themselves.''\par \par \uc2 Though the bill offers exceptions for victims of rape and incest, and in cases of fatal fetal abnormalities or where the woman's life and health are at risk, those exceptions are only available up to 12 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The governor had called a rare special session of the legislature to try to pass a ban, seeking to resolve a standoff between the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 While both chambers are controlled by Republicans, the House is more conservative and had pushed three times to get the Senate to pass a bill banning almost all abortions starting at conception. Three times, the women in the Senate and three Republican male colleagues successfully filibustered. The Republican women argued instead for a 12-week ban, or to put the question to voters in a ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 Their Republican colleagues in the Senate rejected that 12-week proposal, with Senator Richard Cash saying it would lead to ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand'' in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Two of the Republican women had agreed, as a compromise, to a six-week ban with exceptions for medical emergencies, fatal fetal diagnoses and cases of rape and incest. The Senate passed that bill, but because the House added amendments it had to vote again.\par \par \uc2 The women had warned their House colleagues not to make changes to the bill: ''Don't move a semicolon,'' Senator Sandy Senn, a Republican, said. Instead, the House added amendments that the women said would effectively ban all abortions.\par \par \uc2 The amendments included the requirements for the doctors' visits and ultrasounds, and cut a provision that would have allowed minors until 12 weeks to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or seek a waiver from a judge if they could not get parental consent. Opponents of the bill noted that the state's three \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics currently had a two- or three-week wait for an appointment, and that adding requirements for more visits would mean no one would be able to obtain a legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The House version also added declarations of fact that the State Supreme Court had criticized when it struck down the previous six-week ban. One says that cardiac activity, which can be noticed around six weeks, is a ''key indicator'' that a fetus will result in a live birth. Another says that the state has a ''compelling interest from the beginning of a woman's pregnancy to protect the health of the woman and the life of the unborn child.''\par \par \uc2 The legislators who filibustered argued that this could be seen as a declaration that a fetus is a person, opening the door to a ban at conception. The bill also requires fathers to pay child support starting at conception.\par \par \uc2 It also allows the state board of medical examiners to revoke the medical license of any doctor who violates the law, and allows anyone to file a complaint. Parents of a minor could file a civil suit against a doctor who performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Republican leadership in the legislature had been eager to pass a ban that could challenge the State Supreme Court decision from January. The justice who wrote that decision was the only woman on the bench, and she made ample reference to the expansion of rights for women since Roe was decided in 1973.\par \par \uc2 But she retired shortly after and was replaced by a man, making South Carolina the only state with an all-male high court.\par \par \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, celebrated the bill's passage, thanking South Carolina Republicans for their ''persistence.''\par \par \uc2 ''This measure will save thousands of individuals each year who will enrich the lives of others and the state of South Carolina,'' said Caitlin Connors, the group's Southern regional director.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, including the women who tried to filibuster the bill, had been concerned about the rising number of abortions in the state since other Southern states enacted bans. According to state health officials, roughly half of all abortions in recent months have involved residents of other states.\par \par \uc2 In the days leading up to the debate, Shane Massey, the Republican Senate majority leader, declared that South Carolina had become ''the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  capital of the Southeast.''\par \par \uc2 ''The pro-life members of the Senate believe this is unacceptable,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Before the final vote on Tuesday evening, the six-week ban was fiercely condemned by the Republican women senators and their Democratic colleagues.\par \par \uc2 ''When you wake up, when your sisters wake up, when your daughters wake up, and you want to know who took away your rights, it was the Republicans,'' said Brad Hutto, the Democratic Senate minority leader.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230524ej5o0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
