\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Appeals Court Seems Ready to Curtail Access to  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Abbie VanSickle and Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,606 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A panel of three judges heard arguments in a lawsuit by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups asking courts to order a widely used drug off the market.\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court on Wednesday appeared prepared to rule that the availability of a commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill should be curtailed, showing skepticism of the decisions that the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  has made about the drug.\par \par \uc2 At issue is whether to uphold a preliminary ruling from a federal judge in Texas, who declared in April that the F.D.A.'s 23-year-old approval of the pill, mifepristone, was invalid.\par \par \uc2 From the outset of the two-hour hearing in New Orleans, questions and comments from three judges on the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit reflected criticism of the F.D.A. and a lack of familiarity with medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly how it is prescribed.\par \par \uc2 Although the case is still in its early stages and any decision is likely to be appealed, it could ultimately have profound implications.\par \par \uc2 If the initial judge's ruling is upheld, access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be upended in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal, not just in states with bans and restrictions. The F.D.A.'s regulatory authority over other drugs could be challenged with other lawsuits, and pharmaceutical companies say that uncertainty about the F.D.A.'s role could chill drug development in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The arguments included whether the parties that brought the suit -- a coalition of organizations and doctors who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and do not prescribe the pill -- could show they would suffer actual harm if the medication continued to be available and whether they waited too long to challenge the approval of mifepristone, the first pill in a two-drug regimen.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs claim that mifepristone is unsafe, that the F.D.A. did not follow proper regulatory protocols in approving it in 2000 and that the agency has inappropriately eased restrictions on the pill since then. The government strongly disputes those contentions, citing years of research that mifepristone is safe and arguing that the agency has acted responsibly.\par \par \uc2 The panel, composed of two appointees by President Donald J. Trump, Judges James C. Ho and Cory T. Wilson, and a \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  appointee, Judge Jennifer Walker Elrod, did not issue a decision at the hearing on Wednesday. That will come later, though there is no deadline for the court to decide. Any decision is likely to be appealed, first to the full appellate court and then to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Less than a minute into the presentation by Sarah Harrington, the Justice Department lawyer representing the F.D.A., Judge Ho interrupted to criticize her description of the case as ''an unprecedented and unjustified attack on F.D.A. scientific expertise.''\par \par \uc2 ''I hate to cut you off so early, but you've said unprecedented,'' he said, adding that the appeals court had heard other cases against the F.D.A.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harrington replied that those cases were different and that it is the F.D.A.'s job to determine if drugs are safe enough to be approved. ''It's not a court's role to come in and second-guess that expertise, and no court has ever done that,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Judge Ho suggested she ''just focus on the facts of this case rather than have this sort of F.D.A.-can-do-no-wrong theme.''\par \par \uc2 Central to the arguments on Wednesday is whether the plaintiffs -- four anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors and an umbrella group called the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine -- could show they would suffer actual injuries if access and approval of the pill remains unchanged. Lawyers call this requirement standing.\par \par \uc2 In the hearing, both Ms. Harrington and Jessica Ellsworth, a lawyer for a manufacturer of mifepristone, Danco Laboratories, said that the doctors who are plaintiffs cited no examples of cases in which they were compelled to provide treatment for patients who experienced complications from medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''They've never alleged that any identified plaintiff has been required to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  against their religious or conscience beliefs,'' Ms. Harrington said. She said that even if anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors encountered one of the few patients with a serious complication from mifepristone, they could ask colleagues to treat that patient since state and federal laws give health care providers ''robust conscience protections and religious belief protections'' that allow them to decline to give treatment.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for the plaintiffs, Erin Hawley, said that the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors have been harmed in a way that qualifies them to file suit.\par \par \uc2 They ''are not alleging harm because they're forced to provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she said, adding: ''Their conscience harm is much broader. They allege that they feel complicit in an elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by being forced to complete that procedure.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hawley also sought to portray the government as minimizing concerns about medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is different -- we're talking about ending the life of an unborn child,'' she said, adding, ''This case is much different from a doctor who claims a harm from treating an asthmatic child or a gunshot victim because here the actions are directly traceable to the F.D.A.'s unlawful actions in approving and deregulating mifepristone.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Wilson raised several criticisms of the F.D.A.'s decisions in recent years to ease access to the medication, including allowing delivery of the drug by mail.\par \par \uc2 ''You've made it much more likely that patients are going to go to emergency care or a medical clinic where one of these doctors is a member,'' he said. ''I don't see how you square that circle.''\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think any of that is right, and that hasn't been borne out by the evidence,'' Ms. Harrington replied.\par \par \uc2 Judge Elrod noted that the appeals court had received many friend-of-the-court briefs with impassioned arguments on both sides, including some from women in sexually abusive relationships who have said that ''if you don't give them the pill by mail, then they will never get away because they will be trapped by the abuser.''\par \par \uc2 The judge asked Ms. Hawley how the court should view such cases.\par \par \uc2 ''There are other methods of obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are available to people in the tragic circumstances you detailed,'' Ms. Hawley said, adding: ''This case is not about ending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's about ending a particularly dangerous type of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harrington cited studies that have found the method was extremely safe, adding that ''the rate of serious complications is well under 1 percent.''\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs' brief claimed that the F.D.A. unlawfully approved mifepristone in a flawed process that ''put politics above women's health'' and then made ''politically driven decisions to unlawfully push a dangerous regimen.''\par \par \uc2 The government strongly pushed back in its brief, saying, ''F.D.A.'s actions were amply supported by a record developed over decades of safe and effective use of mifepristone in the United States and around the world.''\par \par \uc2 The agency also argued that the plaintiffs waited too long to bring their case.\par \par \uc2 ''They did not sue until more than two decades after mifepristone's approval,'' the government wrote.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen medical associations filed friend-of-the-court briefs in support of the agency.\par \par \uc2 In one brief, medical associations questioned the reasoning behind a ruling by the federal judge in Texas, Matthew J. Kacsmaryk, saying it relied on ''pseudoscience and on speculation.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Kacsmaryk, they wrote, ignored ''decades of unambiguous analysis supporting the use of mifepristone in miscarriage and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Elrod seemed to take particular exception to the way a brief filed by Danco strongly criticized Judge Kacsmaryk's ruling, including calling it a ''relentless one-sided narrative.''\par \par \uc2 In an unusual exchange, Judge Elrod was sharply critical of the language Danco used in its brief, telling a lawyer for the company, Ms. Ellsworth, that its statements ''attack the district court personally'' and used phrasing that ''we normally don't see from learned counsel.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ellsworth said the statements were meant to capture ''our view that the district court was very far outside the bounds'' and were not intended ''as any sort of personal attack.'' But when the judge repeated her criticism and said she wanted to give Danco ''a chance'' to respond differently, Ms. Ellsworth said that ''with more time, we may have ratcheted down some of that.''\par \par \uc2 After the hearing, the plaintiffs stood by the steps of the federal courthouse.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Christina Francis, an Indiana doctor and the chief executive of the American Association of Pro-Life Obstetricians and Gynecologists who filed an affidavit cited by the judges, said, ''We are seeing women come into emergency rooms after having taken this drug with a large amount of bleeding, and we as physicians, we took an oath to take care of our patients.''\par \par \uc2 Bleeding and cramping are normal side effects of the medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  process, a sign that pregnancy tissue is being expelled, medical experts say.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether she had treated women for side effects from mifepristone, Dr. Francis said she had noticed an increase in such cases since the F.D.A. eased some restrictions on the drug in 2016. She did not cite a number or give specifics.\par \par \uc2 Judges with the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit seemed skeptical of the government's defense of a widely used \uc2  drug. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EMILY KASK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230518ej5i0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
