\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 North Carolina Governor Vetoes 12-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Kelly \par \uc2 600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The News\par \par \uc2 The governor of North Carolina, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , vetoed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that was passed by the state's Republican-led legislature. The bill prohibited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 12 weeks, with some exceptions for rape, incest or to preserve the life and health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The veto by Mr. Cooper, a Democrat, sets him up for a showdown with the legislature, which now has a slim Republican supermajority. That means it has the power to override his veto and enact the ban, if the party can muster enough votes.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of people gathered Saturday morning in Raleigh for Mr. Cooper's ''veto rally'' to watch him sign as a way to call attention to his fight with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''Standing in the way of progress right now is this Republican supermajority legislature that only took 48 hours to turn the clock back 50 years,'' Mr. Cooper said. The crowd around him chanted, ''Veto! Veto!''\par \par \uc2 Why It Matters: North Carolina has been a destination for women seeking abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently legal in North Carolina up to 20 weeks. A 12-week ban would dramatically cut \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 The ban would stand to have an impact well outside of the state. North Carolina has become a haven for women across the South who are seeking abortions and whose home states have banned the procedure. North Carolina had one of the biggest upticks in out-of-state abortions since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 The bill is a consequential test of power of the Republican legislature's new supermajority, achieved when a Democratic House member, Tricia Cotham, joined the Republican Party last month. A few weeks later, she voted in favor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in an apparent change of heart on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Background: Republicans pitch the bill as a compromise, but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters see it as a threat.\par \par \uc2 The 12-week ban is not as restrictive as other bans enacted in conservative states since the overturning of Roe v. Wade. Many states have banned most abortions at any stage of pregnancy or after six weeks, before most women even know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The North Carolina ban that Mr. Cooper vetoed allows a larger window, and broader exceptions. Most abortions take place within the first trimester, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . Republicans in North Carolina have pitched the 12-week ban as a compromise.\par \par \uc2 Senator Phil Berger, a Republican, said Saturday that the bill was ''a mainstream approach to limiting elective abortions.''\par \par \uc2 But those who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say the bill would be disastrous for women's health because of other barriers it creates, like longer waiting periods, more in-person doctor visits and restrictions on who can provide abortions.\par \par \uc2 What's Next: An override vote is expected soon.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in the House and Senate are expected to hold votes to override the governor's veto in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Berger, the Republican Senator, and Tim Moore, the House Speaker, both promised a swift override of Mr. Cooper's veto.\par \par \uc2 That override would most likely require the vote of every elected Republican, which is not assured.\par \par \uc2 Governor Cooper has spent days campaigning around the state asking residents to pressure several Republican lawmakers who are viewed as movable on the issue to vote against the override.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Roy Cooper of North Carolina on Saturday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY TRAVIS LONG/THE NEWS & OBSERVER, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230514ej5e0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
