\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access Wins Elections, but Advocates Can't Agree on a Strategy\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Leonhardt and Lyna Bentahar \par \uc2 1,741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 But advocates can't agree on a strategy to hold more ballot initiatives.\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has fared very well when it has appeared on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Voters in California, Michigan and Vermont approved \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights amendments to their state constitutions last year. In Kansas and Kentucky -- both red states -- voters rejected measures that could have led to bans. In Wisconsin this year, the candidate favoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access easily won a judicial election focused on the issue.\par \par \uc2 The 2024 elections offer supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights an opportunity to continue their winning streak. There are 10 states that both significantly restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  (or may soon) and allow citizen-sponsored ballot initiatives, including Florida, Ohio and Arizona. Placing measures on the ballots there offers progressives a possible double victory -- to expand access and energize 2024 turnout among Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 But it remains unclear how many of these states will hold referendums.\par \par \uc2 Advocates have not yet collected the signatures they need in most states, nor have they started a national fund-raising campaign for the effort. At this point, some observers say they would be surprised if even five of the 10 states held initiatives next year.\par \par \uc2 The situation has the potential to be a major missed opportunity for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights movement. One progressive activist told us it would be ''political malpractice.''\par \par \uc2 What explains the apparent lack of urgency? In interviews, some activists said that placing a measure on a ballot took time and money, and that they wanted to make sure the initiatives used language that would survive legal challenges. ''You don't get two bites at this apple,'' Sarah Standiford of \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Other advocates pointed to internal disagreements and disorganization in the movement that have delayed action. The movement has not been able to agree on a national strategy, including whether ballot initiatives should use the word ''women'' and how far into pregnancy \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should remain legal.\par \par \uc2 ''There is just not the command and control that other movements have had,'' another progressive activist said. In the push for same-sex marriage, by contrast, a few major donors and activists played a coordinating role and insisted on urgency.\par \par \uc2 (Related: ''Many \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive-rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates still believe that Planned Parenthood's agenda is too narrow and too cautious,'' Eyal Press writes in The New Yorker. Audm subscribers can listen to that article.)\par \par \uc2 In the rest of today's newsletter, we look more closely at the 10 states.\par \par \uc2 Furthest along\par \par \uc2 Initiatives seem most likely to happen soon in two states.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, a coalition of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups is collecting signatures to place an initiative on the ballot this year that would protect access through roughly the first 24 weeks of pregnancy. For now, most abortions remain legal in Ohio, thanks to a court ruling blocking a ban.\par \par \uc2 In South Dakota, organizers are optimistic that they can collect the signatures needed for an initiative next year. It is a compromise measure that would seek to reinstate the minimum access required by the Roe v. Wade decision. All abortions would be legal in the first trimester (roughly 12 weeks), and some would be in the second trimester.\par \par \uc2 Early efforts\par \par \uc2 In several other states, efforts have begun, but they're less advanced.\par \par \uc2 In Missouri, advocates have not yet settled on one approach. Some petitions -- an early step to putting an initiative on the ballot -- would protect most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access until 24 weeks of pregnancy. Others would be narrower and let the state enact parental consent laws.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, where Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  recently signed a near-total ban, advocates are hoping to place an initiative on the ballot that would allow most abortions until 24 weeks. But the state's pro-marijuana movement helps highlight the slowness of the effort: Organizers of a 2024 ballot initiative to legalize marijuana have already raised $30 million and collected nearly all the required signatures -- while organizers of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiative are just getting started.\par \par \uc2 Arizona has tough rules for ballot initiatives, requiring hundreds of thousands of signatures on a petition. ''That said, we think this fight is more than worth it,'' said Ezra Levin, a founder of Indivisible, a progressive group that's part of the effort. Levin explained that an initiative could both protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and generate enough Democratic turnout to help President Biden win an important swing state.\par \par \uc2 There appears to be less activity in several other states: Arkansas, Montana, Nebraska, North Dakota or Oklahoma. (In Montana, as in Ohio, court rulings have so far prevented bans from taking effect.)\par \par \uc2 In Oklahoma, activists submitted language for a petition last year, but withdrew it a few months later, saying they needed more preparation.\par \par \uc2 Counterattacks\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents aren't sitting still. In several states, they are trying to reduce the chances an initiative can pass, as our colleagues Kate Zernike and Michael Wines have explained.\par \par \uc2 In both Missouri and Ohio, Republican officials want to change the law so any ballot initiative will need 60 percent of the vote to pass. (Florida has had such a law for almost two decades.) Missouri officials are also pushing for ballot language that would summarize an initiative as allowing ''dangerous, unregulated and unrestricted'' abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''There's one reason for all of this,'' said Mallory Schwarz, the executive director of Pro-Choice Missouri, referring to the opponents. ''They know \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is popular.''\par \par \uc2 The bottom line: Polls show that most residents of red and purple states support some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions -- but most also support some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, opinion has shifted left.\par \par \uc2 Even in many conservative states, ballot initiatives to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would have a good chance of receiving 50 percent of the vote. And 60 percent is not out of the question.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, some states may not hold initiatives anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 For more: These Times maps track \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in every state.\par \par \uc2 THE LATEST NEWS\par \par \uc2 Santos Indictment\par \par \uc2 Representative George Santos, a Republican from Long Island, was indicted on federal charges including wire fraud and money laundering.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors charged Santos in three schemes. One involves pocketing donations to a fake super PAC.\par \par \uc2 He pleaded not guilty and plans to stay in office.\par \par \uc2 Politics\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  revived lies about the 2020 election and praised Jan. 6 rioters at an unruly CNN town hall. Here's a fact check.\par \par \uc2 The E.P.A. will announce the first regulations to limit pollution from existing power plants.\par \par \uc2 Biden is wooing moderate Republicans in the debt ceiling standoff, trying to convince them to break away from Speaker Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  returned to the Senate, after missing more than two months with an illness.\par \par \uc2 The End of Title 42\par \par \uc2 A rule that let the U.S. expel migrants quickly expires today. ''Our border is not open,'' the homeland security secretary said.\par \par \uc2 Anticipating an influx of migrants, New York City is temporarily loosening regulations that protect homeless families.\par \par \uc2 Other Big Stories\par \par \uc2 ''I never felt like we acknowledged those who we lost'': Families bereaved by Covid are struggling with the end of the health emergency.\par \par \uc2 Inflation slowed for the 10th straight month. Prices have still risen about 5 percent over the past year.\par \par \uc2 The benefits of making a birth control pill available without prescription outweigh the risks, F.D.A. advisers say.\par \par \uc2 A pancreatic cancer vaccine showed promising results in a small trial.\par \par \uc2 Opinions\par \par \uc2 The pandemic's social safety net expansions are ending. That leaves us unprepared for the next health crisis, Jeneen Interlandi argues.\par \par \uc2 The TV journalist Connie Chung inspired a generation of Asian American women named after her. Connie Wang tells their stories.\par \par \uc2 Times Opinion writers discuss the news on ''Matter of Opinion,'' a new podcast. Their first subject: \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's ethics.\par \par \uc2 MORNING READS\par \par \uc2 Open water: Frigid temperatures and amazing views are part of a 40-mile swim through Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Sweet contraband: Americans are smuggling Fruit Roll-Ups into Israel.\par \par \uc2 QVC 2.0: Will American consumers embrace live shopping online?\par \par \uc2 Summer is coming: Try these bug repellents.\par \par \uc2 Advice from Wirecutter: Clean your towels every three days.\par \par \uc2 Lives Lived: Heather Armstrong, the breakout star behind the website Dooce, was hailed as the queen of the so-called mommy bloggers. She gave millions of readers intimate glimpses of her joys and challenges in parenthood and marriage. She died at 47.\par \par \uc2 SPORTS NEWS FROM THE ATHLETIC\par \par \uc2 N.B.A. playoffs: The \uc2 Knicks\uc2  and the Warriors staved off elimination with home wins.\par \par \uc2 N.H.L. playoffs: The \uc2 Toronto Maple Leafs\uc2  stayed alive in Game 4 of their second-round series against the \uc2 Florida Panthers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Where are the stats? Nearly three years ago, M.L.B. announced an initiative to incorporate Negro League statistics into official record. There has been little progress.\par \par \uc2 ARTS AND IDEAS\par \par \uc2 A definitive biography\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Eig's new biography of \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . is ''penetrating, heartstring-pulling and compulsively readable,'' the Times critic Dwight Garner writes. Eig told The Times: ''We'd turned him into a monument and a national holiday and lost sight of his humanity. So I really wanted to write a more intimate book.''\par \par \uc2 High praise: In The Spectator, David Garrow -- author of the last major King biography -- makes the case for Eig's book as the definitive one.\par \par \uc2 PLAY, WATCH, EAT\par \par \uc2 What to Cook\par \par \uc2 Make toast in a skillet and top it with cheesy eggs.\par \par \uc2 What to Read\par \par \uc2 ''Paved Paradise,'' by Henry Grabar, examines the U.S. obsession with parking.\par \par \uc2 Concerts\par \par \uc2 Beyonc\u233\'00\'E9 opened her first solo tour in seven years in Stockholm. She left most of the choreography to her dancers.\par \par \uc2 Now Time to Play\par \par \uc2 The pangram from yesterday's Spelling Bee was zoological. Here are today's puzzle and the Bee Buddy, which helps you find remaining words.\par \par \uc2 And here are today's Mini Crossword, Wordle and Sudoku.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for spending part of your morning with The Times. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 P.S. Krista Mahr joins Times Opinion as its deputy international editor.\par \par \uc2 Here's today's front page.\par \par \uc2 Sign up here to get this newsletter in your inbox . Reach our team at themorning@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 An Ohio woman signing a petition to put a constitutional amendment protecting \uc2  rights on the ballot in November. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MADDIE MCGARVEY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230514ej5e0004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
