\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Trump Takes Stances on Jan. 6 Pardons and Migrant Children\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Vigdor \par \uc2 1,197 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  staked out positions on several major issues, including separating migrant children from their parents and pardoning Jan. 6 rioters.\par \par \uc2 Among the barrage of falsehoods and bluster, former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  laid markers down on several major and divisive issues at the CNN town-hall meeting on Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump spoke of several actions he might take if re-elected, at times with a specificity he often dodges in speeches and friendlier interviews. He also revealed much about his thinking on positions that are likely to roil his party, including the war in Ukraine and access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at some of what Mr. Trump said about policy:\par \par \uc2 Reconsidering migrant family separations\par \par \uc2 When asked if he would return to a policy of separating migrant children from their parents when they arrive at the border, Mr. Trump did not rule it out.\par \par \uc2 ''Well, when you have that policy, people don't come,'' he said. ''If a family hears that they're going to be separated, they love their family, they don't come.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump acknowledged that the policy ''sounds harsh'' but claimed that the situation warranted it.\par \par \uc2 Some 5,500 foreign-born children, and hundreds of U.S. citizens, are known to have been separated from their parents under the Trump administration's so-called zero tolerance policy, which jailed and criminally charged migrant parents for crossing the border without authorization.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump abandoned the policy after an international outcry in 2018.\par \par \uc2 President Biden formed a commission to reunite parents with their children, some of whom have spent years in foster care. He also vowed not to separate families at the border and quickly ended the detention of families, though the administration is considering new efforts such as curfews and the use of more GPS monitors for adults as they see more surges of families arriving at the border.\par \par \uc2 Pardons for the Jan. 6 rioters\par \par \uc2 When asked if he had any regrets about his actions leading up to the riot at the U.S. Capitol on Jan. 6, 2021, Mr. Trump insisted that he did nothing wrong and sympathized with his supporters who took part.\par \par \uc2 A retired lawyer in the audience asked Mr. Trump if he would issue pardons to those rioters who were convicted of federal offenses.\par \par \uc2 ''I am inclined to pardon many of them,'' Mr. Trump said. ''I can't say for every single one because a couple of them, probably, they got out of control.''\par \par \uc2 More than 900 people have been criminally charged as part of the assault on the Capitol, including four members of the far-right group the Proud Boys, who were convicted this month of sedition.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump did not rule out pardons for them, saying he would have to review their individual circumstances.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't know,'' he said. ''I'd have to look at their case, but I will say in Washington, D.C., you cannot get a fair trial, you cannot. Just like in New York City, you can't get a fair trial either.''\par \par \uc2 Dodging on a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump repeatedly sidestepped questions about whether he would sign a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if Republicans managed to steer one through the divided Congress. He also would not say how many weeks into a pregnancy he might consider banning an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I'm looking at a solution that's going to work,'' he said. ''Very complex issue for the country. You have people on both sides of an issue, but we are now in a very strong position. Pro-life people are in a strong position to make a deal that's going to be good and going to be satisfactory for them.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump appointed three conservative justices to the Supreme Court during his presidency, paving the way for the court to eliminate the federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But he has since resisted being drawn into the debate, and has privately worried about political backlash.\par \par \uc2 Characterizing his views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as similar to President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's, Mr. Trump said that he believed in exceptions for rape, for incest and to save the life of a mother.\par \par \uc2 Not taking Ukraine's side\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump skirted the issue when asked multiple times if he wanted Ukraine to win the war after being invaded last year by Russia.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think in terms of winning and losing,'' he said. ''I think in terms of getting it settled so we stop killing all these people.''\par \par \uc2 The former president claimed he would bring the war to an end in 24 hours, if he returned to office, but did not specifically say what he would do to broker a peace.\par \par \uc2 He would not call President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  of Russia a war criminal, as Mr. Biden has, saying that doing so would make it more difficult to end the hostilities between the two nations.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump did say Mr. Putin had ''made a bad mistake'' by invading Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Threatening default on U.S. debt\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump suggested on Wednesday night that Republicans in Congress should hold fast against raising the federal debt ceiling without budget cuts, even if it means the country defaults on its debt.\par \par \uc2 ''I say to the Republicans out there -- congressmen, senators -- if they don't give you massive cuts, you're going to have to do a default,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 A growing list of economists and analysts have warned about the potential consequences if Congress does not raise the borrowing limit before the government can no longer pay its bills, including huge job losses, a recession and a nosedive on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump predicted that Democrats would ''absolutely cave'' when confronted with the choice between accepting spending cuts and defaulting. Still, when asked to clarify if he would endorse a default, he said he would.\par \par \uc2 ''We might as well do it now because you'll do it later,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Collins pointed out that Mr. Trump had once said when he was president that using the debt ceiling as a negotiating wedge could not happen, he said that circumstances had changed.\par \par \uc2 ''Because now I'm not president,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The Big Lie 2.0?\par \par \uc2 On a night when he doubled and tripled down on his false claims that the 2020 election was rigged, Mr. Trump refused to say unconditionally that he would accept the results of next year's election should he become the Republican presidential nominee.\par \par \uc2 ''If I think it's an honest election, I would be honored to,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump spent much of the interview re-litigating his defeat and closed with a caveat about the next election.\par \par \uc2 ''If it's an honest election, correct, I will,'' he said of accepting the results.\par \par \uc2 Alyce McFadden and Michael D. Shear contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Alyce McFadden and Michael D. Shear contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump laid out specific actions he might take if re-elected during CNN's town hall in New Hampshire on Wednesday night. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CNN) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document