\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Sued for Helping a Friend Obtain An  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , 2 Women Sue Back\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emily Bazelon \par \uc2 1,040 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A Texas man says text messages on his ex-wife's phone show the women helped her obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The women say he invaded their privacy.\par \par \uc2 In March, a Texas man, Marcus Silva, sued three women for $1 million each after they helped his ex-wife obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last summer using pills. The suit alleged that the termination of the pregnancy qualified as wrongful death under state law, and he presented text messages between his ex-wife and the women as evidence.\par \par \uc2 In the post-Roe era, the suit horrified \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates and galvanized opponents. Both sides view it as a test case aimed at discouraging anyone from helping women access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states where the procedure is now banned or severely restricted.\par \par \uc2 This week, two of the women, Jackie Noyola and Amy Carpenter, filed their response in court: They are countersuing Mr. Silva for invasion of privacy in addition to offering a number of defenses to his claims. Ms. Noyola and Ms. Carpenter, who are close friends of Brittni Silva, Mr. Silva's ex-wife, said he searched her phone without her consent and read their private messages.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates have raised the alarm over how private information might be used in both civil and criminal cases against people who have abortions, and those who help them. In Nebraska, prosecutors used \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages between a mother and daughter to bring charges against them after the daughter's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Noyola and Ms. Carpenter, the experience of being sued has been difficult, said their lawyer, Rusty Hardin. ''They're being thrust into the public arena, with their financial well-being at risk,'' he said. ''They believed strongly that they were helping a friend at a time of dire need.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Silva's lawyer, Jonathan Mitchell, a former solicitor general of Texas, has advocated using private lawsuits to deter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He was the architect of the Texas law, passed in 2021, which led clinics in the state to stop providing abortions after six weeks, by deputizing private citizens to enforce the law by suing for cash judgments of $10,000 per procedure. Mr. Mitchell did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mitchell's argument in this case uses the state's wrongful death statute to seek damages on behalf of Mr. Silva.\par \par \uc2 The suit asserts that under that state law, the rights of a fetus are equivalent to those of an adult. If Mr. Mitchell's argument succeeds, it would be a legal victory for the concept of fetal personhood -- a goal of many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Silva's complaint is based on a series of photos he took of texts between Ms. Noyola, Ms. Carpenter, and Ms. Silva. (The third woman he sued, who he says supplied the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to his wife, has not yet responded in court and could not be reached for comment.)\par \par \uc2 According to the texts, Ms. Silva's friends helped her date her pregnancy, which was early in the first trimester, and figure out how to get pills to terminate it.\par \par \uc2 ''Jackie your help means the world to me,'' Ms. Silva wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Silva is not suing his ex-wife. Her friends' countersuit includes a police report Mr. Silva filed in which he admitted that he ''went through his wife's phone.''\par \par \uc2 ''There are clear violations of Texas law in this case with respect to illegally accessing the phone,'' said Charles Rhodes, a law professor at \uc2 South Texas College of Law Houston\uc2 . ''And that's a situation which will probably occur in other cases.''\par \par \uc2 Without a private message, or evidence like a \uc2 Google\uc2  search that another person might well access without permission, Rhodes asks, ''how else would you have the evidence you need to bring your lawsuit?''\par \par \uc2 In their countersuit, Ms. Noyola and Ms. Carpenter included more texts -- provided, their lawyers said, with Ms. Silva's consent. Ms. Silva declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Silva claimed that Mr. Silva had a history of emotionally abusive behavior. In her texts to her friends, she recounted that he burned their wedding photos and threatened the family dog. Ms. Silva told her friends once that she called the police because he was harassing her.\par \par \uc2 She filed for divorce in May 2022, but continued living with Mr. Silva.\par \par \uc2 In his suit, Mr. Silva says he only recently learned of his ex-wife's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But in a police report he filed on July 18, 2022, he stated that he found the text messages on July 12, searched her purse the next day, and found an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill. He put the pill back. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place on July 14. Mr. Silva waited to confront Ms. Silva about it.\par \par \uc2 ''So now he's saying if I don't give him my 'mind body and soul' until the end of the divorce, which he's going to drag out, he's going to make sure I go to jail for doing it,'' Ms. Silva wrote to her friends on July 23.\par \par \uc2 The case has other legal complexities. It is a crime in Texas to provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to someone else but not to self-administer an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at issue in Mr. Silva's suit took place before Texas' ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in effect. ''The woman here was clearly within her rights to end her own pregnancy, which means this would be a very unusual application of the wrongful death statute,'' said Joanna Grossman, a law professor at \uc2 Southern Methodist University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rhodes, on the other hand, thinks that ''the intent of the Texas wrongful-death statute, when it was amended in 2003, was to allow wrongful-death claims against individuals who assisted with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that wasn't done legally.''\par \par \uc2 None of the women in Mr. Silva's suit will face criminal charges, Jack Roady, the district attorney in Galveston County, the relevant jurisdiction, said in March.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \uc2  rights in Amarillo, Texas. Two women say their friend's ex-husband searched her phone without consent. (PHOTOGRAPH BY NICK OXFORD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230505ej550003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
