\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Test of Supermajority, North Carolina Legislature Passes 12-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen and Kate Kelly \par \uc2 970 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans used their new supermajority status to push through the restriction, which stands to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access throughout the South if enacted.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina hastily approved legislation on Thursday that would ban most abortions after 12 weeks of pregnancy, setting the stage for a likely test of the Republican Party's new, but slim, supermajority.\par \par \uc2 After an emotional, five-hour debate, the Senate, by a vote of 29-20, approved a ban the House had already passed the night before.\par \par \uc2 The bill now goes to the state's Democratic governor, who has called it ''extreme,'' and said he would veto it. He has 10 days to act.\par \par \uc2 But the legislature has the potential to override his veto if Republicans can keep their party united to muster enough votes.\par \par \uc2 In a video he posted to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  on Thursday, the governor, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , urged residents to help uphold his veto by pressuring four Republicans who had campaigned to protect women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  to break from their party.\par \par \uc2 If enacted, the bill would reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state, which currently allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 20 weeks. It would also further curtail access throughout the South. North Carolina has become a destination for women seeking abortions as one state after another has pushed to ban or restrict the procedure since the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade last summer.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear when a vote to override the governor's expected veto might occur. And legislators may not vote the same way they did on the bill; in the past, some have deliberately walked off the chamber floor in hopes of preventing an override vote.\par \par \uc2 The absence during Wednesday night's House vote of Representative Ted Davis Jr., a Republican, suggested that at least one G.O.P. vote is in play. Mr. Davis did not respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 To overturn the governor's veto, at least 60 percent of lawmakers present must vote in favor of an override. If all legislators are in attendance, that would require all 72 Republicans in the House and all 30 in the Senate to vote for the override.\par \par \uc2 Framed as a ''mainstream'' compromise by Republicans, the bill is not nearly as severe as bans that have been embraced by conservative states. Most abortions take place before 13 weeks, according to the C.D.C.\par \par \uc2 Yet the North Carolina ban has outraged those in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, who say it erects significant hurdles for those seeking abortions. On Thursday morning, protesters looped around the General Assembly building in the capital city of Raleigh. Around 5 p.m., when the Senate passed the bill, viewers in the chamber gallery erupted, shouting ''Shame! Shame!''\par \par \uc2 The bill could also impose new licensing requirements for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that Planned Parenthood South Atlantic, which operates six clinics in the state, said could cause facilities to close.\par \par \uc2 The bill would also require at least two in-person visits to doctors, which would have a disproportionate impact on women with limited finances, transportation options, or job flexibility.\par \par \uc2 Exceptions for rape or incest would be permitted up to 20 weeks. If a doctor determines that a fetus has a ''life-limiting anomaly,'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is permitted up to 24 weeks. What the bill describes as ''eugenic abortions'' due to sex, race or Down syndrome would be prohibited.\par \par \uc2 And in a nod to conservatives who have sought to provide more financial support for families, the bill would dedicate at least $160 million for child care, contraceptives and foster care, among other initiatives.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists applauded the measure, even though they had pushed for a six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 ''We are grateful more babies will be protected,'' said \uc2 Tami Fitzgerald\uc2 , executive director of the NC Values Coalition. Passage of the bill, she said on Thursday, ''marks the end of North Carolina as a destination for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is a historic step forward for unborn babies and their mothers.''\par \par \uc2 During the House debate Wednesday night, Representative Julie von Haefen, a Democrat, said: ''Make no mistake: your actions today will harm women.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Senator Joyce Krawiec, a Republican who helped negotiate the package, called the measures ''pro-woman.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans have long controlled the legislature but have been thwarted by Mr. Cooper, who has vetoed more than 75 measures since taking office in 2017. Yet last month, a longtime Democrat, Representative Tricia Cotham, unexpectedly switched parties, giving Republicans narrow supermajorities in both chambers.\par \par \uc2 When she was still a Democrat, Ms. Cotham promised to help codify Roe v. Wade in her state. Yet she voted in favor of the 12-week ban on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also objected to the process being rushed, with no opportunity for public review.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's debate over the bill was the Senate's longest in at least a decade, legislators said.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jonas Swartz, a Duke Health obstetrician and gynecologist who has been following the policy discussions closely, said that elements like the need for patients to receive counseling in person 72 hours before an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than over the phone, would make the procedure less accessible for many.\par \par \uc2 ''Even though they had discussed that 12-week ban, this is worse than what I expected,'' he said. ''I think it will be really difficult for people who live in a county without an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in North Carolina to arrange for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and even more difficult for people who live out of state, which is probably the point.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats in the North Carolina Senate voting against a bill that would establish new limits on \uc2  in the state. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ETHAN HYMAN/THE NEWS & OBSERVER, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230505ej5500039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
