\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justice's Private Papers Open Window Into Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,180 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 John Paul Stevens\uc2 's files on thousands of cases, including landmark decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the 2000 election, have been made public, opening a window on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- In June 1992, less than two weeks before the Supreme Court reaffirmed the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established in Roe v. Wade, Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2  sent a colleague some ''late-night musings.''\par \par \uc2 ''Roe was, at the least, a very close case,'' Justice Kennedy wrote in the three-page memorandum, which included reflections on the power of precedent, the court's legitimacy and the best way to address a cutting dissent.\par \par \uc2 The document is part of an enormous trove of the private papers of Justice \uc2 John Paul Stevens\uc2  released on Tuesday by the \uc2 Library of Congress\uc2 . They provide a panoramic inside look at the justices at work on thousands of cases, including Bush v. Gore and the 1992 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, Planned Parenthood v. Casey.\par \par \uc2 The papers are studded with candid and occasionally caustic remarks, sometimes echoing current concerns about the court's power and authority.\par \par \uc2 In the Casey decision, Justice Kennedy joined a controlling opinion with Justices \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2  and David H. Souter that saved the core of the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established in Roe in 1973.\par \par \uc2 In June, the current Supreme Court overturned Roe and Casey after considering questions about precedent and the court's legitimacy, coming to the opposite conclusion from Justice Kennedy.\par \par \uc2 There are other echoes of recent events in the papers of Justice Stevens, who served on the court for 35 years, retired in 2010 and died in 2019, at 99.\par \par \uc2 There was, for instance, an apparent leak, one that prompted Chief Justice William H. Rehnquist to write a stern note to all of the law clerks on June 10, 1992. The current issue of Newsweek, the chief justice wrote, ''contains a purported account of what is happening inside the court in the case of Planned Parenthood v. Casey.''\par \par \uc2 The article, attributing its information to ''sources'' and ''clerks,'' said that ''at least three of the nine justices are planning to draft opinions in Casey'' and predicted, correctly, that the decision would be released on June 29.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Rehnquist admonished the clerks to follow a rule in the court's code of conduct, which said, ''There should be as little communication as possible between the clerk and representatives of the press.'' He added, underlining the last three words: ''In the case of any matter pending before the court, the least possible communication is none at all.''\par \par \uc2 Researchers will be studying the Stevens papers for decades, and only small glimpses were possible in a day's scrutiny of a selection of them. But those glimpses made clear that the current turmoil at the court has historical analogues.\par \par \uc2 In 2000, for instance, when the court handed the presidency to \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  in Bush v. Gore by a 5-to-4 vote, members of the majority wrote scathing private memos protesting what they called unduly harsh language in the dissents.\par \par \uc2 Justice Stevens's dissent ended this way: ''Although we may never know with complete certainty the identity of the winner of this year's presidential election, the identity of the loser is perfectly clear. It is the nation's confidence in the judge as an impartial guardian of the rule of law.''\par \par \uc2 In a memo to his colleagues on Dec. 12, 2000, the day the decision was issued, Justice Kennedy, who had voted with the majority, appeared wounded.\par \par \uc2 ''The tone of the dissents is disturbing both on an institutional and personal level,'' he wrote. ''I have agonized over this and made my best judgment.''\par \par \uc2 He added, ''The dissents, permit me to say, in effect try to coerce the majority by trashing the court themselves, thereby making their dire, and I think unjustified, predictions a self-fulfilling prophecy.''\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , who had also voted with the majority, said he was ''the last person to complain that dissents should not be thorough and hard hitting.''\par \par \uc2 But he said he could not ''help but observe that those of my colleagues who were protesting so vigorously that the court's judgment today will do irreparable harm have spared no pains -- in a veritable blizzard of separate dissents -- to assist that result.''\par \par \uc2 At an earlier stage of the case, Justice \uc2 Stephen G. Breyer\uc2 , who dissented in Bush v. Gore, urged his colleagues to stay away from the dispute, recalling the role that Supreme Court justices had played on a commission created to resolve the contested presidential election of 1876.\par \par \uc2 ''Rather than the court lending the process legitimacy, the process damaged the legitimacy of the court,'' Justice Breyer wrote. ''I doubt very much that our intervention would assure anyone that the process had worked more fairly. Rather, I fear that history could repeat itself, were we to intervene now.''\par \par \uc2 In statements after the Supreme Court's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . has said that attacks on the court's legitimacy, as opposed to its reasoning, should be out of bounds.\par \par \uc2 In the 1992 memo containing his ''late-night musings,'' which was addressed to Justice Souter and copied to Justices O'Connor and Stevens, Justice Kennedy also reflected on the court's legitimacy in the context of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He appeared troubled by aspects of Chief Justice Rehnquist's dissent, which said public opinion should not affect the court's work.\par \par \uc2 ''You can fend off the chief,'' Justice Kennedy told Justice Souter, ''by stating that we are not concerned with preserving our legitimacy for our own sake but for the sake of the Constitution. Thus, when we speak of the principled character of our decisions, we mean that they are informed by precedent, logic and the traditions of our people, all with reference to our constitutional heritage.''\par \par \uc2 ''We must be clear,'' he went on, ''that we are not guided by expediency, contemporary attitudes or our own morality.''\par \par \uc2 The newly released files cover the years up to 2005, when Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . joined the Supreme Court. They are filled with notes in Justice Stevens's not always legible scrawl, marked-up briefs, draft opinions, vote tallies, memos among the justices, recommendations from clerks and all manner of other paperwork.\par \par \uc2 Before the new release, the most recent set of Supreme Court papers was from the files of Justice Harry A. Blackmun, who served through 1994 and died in 1999.\par \par \uc2 The only current member of the court featured in the new files is Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . The remaining parts of Justice Stevens's papers are scheduled to be released in 2030.\par \par \uc2 Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Kitty Bennett contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Justice John Paul Stevens's notes from Bush v. Gore. The papers, released this week by the Library of Congress, sometimes echo current concerns about the Supreme Court's power and authority. (PHOTOGRAPH BY J. SCOTT APPLEWHITE/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230503ej530003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
