\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Clinics in Utah Are Kept Open by Judge\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen \par \uc2 691 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The judge temporarily blocked a new law would have required the procedure be performed in hospitals, and effectively restricted access across the state, clinic operators say.\par \par \uc2 A Utah state judge on Tuesday temporarily blocked a new law, one day before it was scheduled to take effect, that would have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and potentially put a halt to most abortions in the state.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in Utah up to 18 weeks of pregnancy, and after that only in limited circumstances. But legislators have been trying in recent years to further restrict the procedure. A state law which would ban nearly all abortions is suspended while the Utah Supreme Court considers whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is protected in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 While the more stringent ban is temporarily blocked by the legal challenge, the Legislature, dominated by Republicans, passed another bill known as H.B. 467 that was signed into law by the Republican governor and zeroed in on something else: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, where 95 percent of all abortions are performed in the state.\par \par \uc2 Among other provisions, the law makes it a crime to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  anywhere other than a hospital. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics would lose their licenses if they performed the procedure. And even if they stopped performing abortions, no new licenses would be issued after May.\par \par \uc2 The law, signed by Gov. \uc2 Spencer Cox\uc2  in March, was scheduled to take effect on Wednesday. The Planned Parenthood Association of Utah -- which runs three of the state's four clinics -- sued, arguing that the law would all but eliminate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that it was designed to circumvent the pending case on the more stringent ban.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Judge Andrew H. Stone of Utah's Third Judicial District agreed with Planned Parenthood. In his 22-page ruling, he wrote that the organization had offered evidence suggesting that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics had been unreasonably singled out, and that the state's rationale for the new law was ''nebulous.''\par \par \uc2 ''There is nothing before the court to indicate that an injunction would be adverse to the public interest,'' the judge wrote.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Sean D. Reyes, Utah's attorney general, declined to comment, citing pending litigation.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Sarah Stoesz, interim president and chief executive of the Planned Parenthood Association of Utah, said: ''While we welcome this victory, the threat to Utahns' health and personal freedom remains dire as politicians continue to undermine our judicial process and fight the injunction against Utah's trigger ban.''\par \par \uc2 The practical effect, for now, is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services can continue uninterrupted at the state's four clinics, three of which are in the metropolitan Salt Lake City area.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals in Utah typically do not provide abortions outside of extenuating circumstances, such as when the patient's health is seriously threatened, in cases of severe fetal anomalies, or in the rare instances when accusations of rape or incest are reported to the police, said Hannah Swanson, a lawyer for Planned Parenthood. Public money cannot be used to finance abortions, and neither can private or public insurance.\par \par \uc2 So if the law had gone into effect, Ms. Swanson said, then people from the Salt Lake City area seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services would have had to drive some 360 miles to the nearest clinic in Glenwood Springs, Colo.\par \par \uc2 Residents in the less-populated southern part of the state would have had to go to Las Vegas, or other places in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Still, Judge Stone's ruling is not the final word. Planned Parenthood challenged the state's broader ban after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade last summer.\par \par \uc2 In that case, the plaintiffs contend that the law violates the right to privacy as laid out in the Utah Constitution, and the right to determine family composition, among other arguments.\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators outside the Utah State Capitol in Salt Lake City after Roe v. Wade was overturned last June. \uc2  is still legal there up to 18 weeks, and in limited circumstances after that. (PHOTOGRAPH BY KIM RAFF FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230503ej530003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
