\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 North Dakota Enacts Strict Ban on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani \par \uc2 755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The governor approved the state's prohibition on Monday, just a month after the State Supreme Court blocked an earlier ban.\par \par \uc2 North Dakota became the latest state on Monday to enact a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just one month after the State Supreme Court temporarily blocked a similar ban from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 Under the new law, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the case of rape or incest would be permissible only in the first six weeks of pregnancy, a time when most women have not yet realized they are pregnant. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is permitted without gestational limits if terminating a pregnancy can ''prevent the death or a serious health risk'' of the mother.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , a Republican, signed the bill into law on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The law, Mr. Burgum said, ''reaffirms North Dakota as a pro-life state.'' The governor added that it also ''clarifies and refines'' the existing state ban that has been blocked by courts.\par \par \uc2 The new law, which takes immediate effect, is a dramatic shift for the state, where abortions had been legal up until 22 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Under the earlier ban, providers who performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save the life of a mother could face felony prosecution. The provider would need to offer an ''affirmative defense'' proving that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was medically necessary within the confines of the state law.\par \par \uc2 Under the new version of the law, the exceptions do not require an affirmative defense from providers. But providers could still face criminal charges if they violate the exceptions detailed in the law.\par \par \uc2 The law makes North Dakota at least the 14th state with an active ban on nearly all abortions, though it is likely to face legal challenges, experts said.\par \par \uc2 Since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year, conservative states have enacted restrictions or full bans. Liberal states have gone in the opposite direction, reaffirming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in state constitutions and becoming magnets for women seeking abortions in states with bans.\par \par \uc2 North Dakota's trigger ban was blocked last year by a district judge, after its sole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, the Red River Women's Clinic, filed a lawsuit against the law.\par \par \uc2 The State Supreme Court upheld the lower court's ruling last month and said the state Constitution protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in some situations.\par \par \uc2 ''North Dakota lawmakers are attempting to bypass the state Constitution and court system with this total ban,'' said Elisabeth Smith, director of state policy and advocacy at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which brought the lawsuit on behalf of Red River clinic. ''They made the exceptions a little bit less narrow but essentially tried to repackage the trigger ban.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Smith said the center was still analyzing the new law and had not ''determined what the next steps will be.''\par \par \uc2 Tammi Kromenaker, director of the Red River clinic, called the new law ''an assault on bodily autonomy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.''\par \par \uc2 ''This legislation is out of step with the people of North Dakota,'' Ms. Kromenaker said.\par \par \uc2 The Red River Women's Clinic stopped offering abortions in the state, instead moving a short drive across the border to Moorhead, Minn., in August. But attorneys representing the clinic say it is important to ensure that the ban does not take effect, so that patients facing medical emergencies can receive abortions in hospitals and from their doctors.\par \par \uc2 ''North Dakota has been pro-life before statehood, and before Roe,'' said the bill's sponsor, Senator Janne Myrdal, a Republican. ''I'm passionate about protecting women, but also their unborn children, that goes hand in hand.''\par \par \uc2 Most states with sweeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions offer some exceptions to save the life of a mother, but translating those exceptions has sown confusion and turmoil among doctors and patients. In the months since the overturning of Roe v. Wade, very few exceptions to these new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have been granted, a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  review of available state data and interviews with dozens of physicians, advocates and lawmakers revealed.\par \par \uc2 In last month's opinion, the North Dakota Supreme Court raised concerns that a sweeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban might violate ''a fundamental right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the limited instances of lifesaving and health-preserving circumstances.''\par \par \uc2 Amy Schoenfeld Walker contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Amy Schoenfeld Walker contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Doug Burgum, center, signed the bill Monday, just a month after the State Supreme Court blocked an earlier ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANNA MONEYMAKER/GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230426ej4q00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal426