\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Almost a Year After Roe Reversal,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is the Center of 2024 Races\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,773 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly a year after the Supreme Court turned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into a dominant issue of the 2022 midterms, the battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has catapulted to the center of the emerging 2024 election season, igniting Democrats, dividing Republicans and turbocharging sensitive debates over health care.\par \par \uc2 From North Carolina to Nevada, Democrats running at every level of government are vowing to make support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a pillar of their campaigns, and to paint their opponents as extremists on the issue.\par \par \uc2 And as races intensify, Republicans are caught between the demands of their socially conservative base and a broader American public that generally supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, exposing one of the party's biggest political liabilities as it tries to win back the White House, recapture the Senate and expand its narrow House majority.\par \par \uc2 All of those dynamics have crystallized over the last month. First, a liberal Wisconsin judge won a crucial State Supreme Court race by a commanding margin after running assertively on her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. A few days later, a conservative judge in Texas took the extraordinary step of moving to invalidate the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone. The Supreme Court on Friday said the pill would remain widely available for now, halting two separate rulings, including the Texas ruling, while an appeal moves forward.\par \par \uc2 Democrats cast the Supreme Court's order as a close call, and warned that many Republicans still want as many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as possible, including a national ban. At the same time, Republican presidential hopefuls -- whose teams generally did not respond to requests for comment on the Supreme Court's ruling on Friday night -- are straining to find their footing on the issue.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida recently signed a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy, when many women do not know they are pregnant, staking out a position that conservatives applauded, but one that could hurt him in a general election with moderate voters. Others, like Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina, have struggled to articulate firm positions. And former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , whose choices for the Supreme Court helped overturn Roe v. Wade, recently angered anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders by emphasizing state power over the issue rather than a national ban.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm worried that we let the Democrats use the issue to define us, because we aren't very good at our own messaging,'' said the Republican governor of New Hampshire, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , who signed a measure that banned abortions after 24 weeks, with some exceptions. Mr. Sununu, who calls himself ''pro-choice,'' was the rare possible Republican presidential candidate to offer a comment on the court's ruling on Friday: ''Good call by the Supreme Court.''\par \par \uc2 Representative Suzan DelBene, a Washington Democrat who leads the House Democratic campaign arm, said Republicans had moved in an increasingly ''extreme'' direction on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She pointed, for instance, to an Idaho law criminalizing those who help a minor get an out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without parental permission, and to threats more broadly to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 ''It's dangerous, and people are angry,'' she said. ''We're going to see that in 2024 in elections across the country.''\par \par \uc2 As President Biden moves toward announcing a re-election bid as soon as Tuesday, one of his advisers predicted that the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would be more significant in 2024 than it was last year, as Americans experience the far-reaching results of overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are carefully monitoring -- and eagerly broadcasting -- the positions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  taken by Republicans in the nascent stages of primary season. And they are pressing their own succinct message.\par \par \uc2 ''We support women making decisions regarding their health care,'' said Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , a Michigan Democrat who leads the Democratic Senate campaign arm. ''Not politicians, not judges.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans are far more divided on what their pitch should be -- and party officials acknowledge this poses a steep challenge.\par \par \uc2 Conflict always emerges between the demands of primary voters and the preferences of general-election swing voters. But the overturning of Roe has drastically complicated this calculus for Republican candidates. They now face detailed questions about whether to support national bans; how soon into a pregnancy \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans should apply; what exceptions, if any, to permit; and how they view medication used in instances of abortions and miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 ''We wrap ourselves around the axle trying to nuance our position as a candidate or a party through the primary, knowing that we're going to have to reexplain ourselves in the general,'' Mr. Sununu said. ''It comes off as disingenuous, convoluted, and at the end of the day, it really chases away voters.''\par \par \uc2 The fault lines in the party were illuminated again this past week. After a spokesman for Mr. Trump indicated to The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  that the former president believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be decided at the state level, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America issued a stern rebuke.\par \par \uc2 ''We will oppose any presidential candidate who refuses to embrace at a minimum a 15-week national standard to stop painful late-term abortions while allowing states to enact further protections,'' Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of the organization, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 In a separate statement, Mr. Trump's campaign said he ''believes it is in the states where the greatest advances can now take place to protect the unborn,'' while declaring him the ''most pro-life president in American history.''\par \par \uc2 There will be no shortage of opportunities for Republican candidates to highlight their anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  credentials and to navigate the fallout from the Supreme Court's decision, starting as soon as Saturday, at a gathering of the Iowa Faith and Freedom Coalition. On Tuesday, Nikki Haley, the former United Nations ambassador, is also expected to give a speech on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bob Vander Plaats, a socially conservative leader in Iowa whose organization is expected to host a gathering with presidential candidates this summer, said, ''There's a lot of ways to determine a person's bona fides when it comes to the sanctity of human life, but I guarantee you the Texas ruling will be discussed.''\par \par \uc2 The issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said, ''will be a cornerstone issue in the Iowa caucuses. It will be a cornerstone issue in the Republican primary.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , the chair of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , tried to help her candidates navigate the subject, suggesting that opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy was a strong position politically, somewhat mirroring polling she has been showing to members of her party.\par \par \uc2 ''In 2022, a lot of Republican candidates took their D.C. consultants' bad advice to ignore the subject,'' she said in a speech. Noting the onslaught of Democratic ads on the subject, she said, ''most Republicans had no response.''\par \par \uc2 She urged Republicans to cast Democrats as ''extreme'' on the issue, a message echoed by some working on House and Senate races who say Democrats should be pressed on what limitations they support.\par \par \uc2 Nicole McCleskey, a Republican pollster who worked for the successful re-election campaign of Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  of Iowa last year, pointed to Ms. Reynolds, Gov. \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  of Ohio and Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia as examples of leaders who embraced tight \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions but were not defined by that issue alone. All three swept to comfortable victories in states that often lean right, but are not the nation's most conservative states.\par \par \uc2 ''This last election saw some candidates who were unclear or changed their position, lacked conviction and were unprepared to talk about this issue,'' she said. ''If you have those things -- if you have conviction, if you have empathy, if you are prepared and you know how to define yourself and your opposition,'' she added, ''we can successfully navigate this issue.''\par \par \uc2 But some candidates have shown little interest in managing a rhetorical balancing act.\par \par \uc2 The issue is likely to come to a head in North Carolina, home to what may be the most consequential governor's race of 2024, with Gov. \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democrat, term-limited.\par \par \uc2 Mark Robinson, the state's often incendiary lieutenant governor and a Republican, announced a run for governor Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Robinson, who has said that he and his now-wife aborted a pregnancy decades ago, has since made clear that he wants far greater restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in North Carolina, casting doubt on the need for exceptions in cases of rape and incest. The procedure is currently legal up to 20 weeks of pregnancy in the state, where Republicans have supermajorities in the legislature.\par \par \uc2 Josh Stein\uc2 , the state's Democratic attorney general who is running for governor, said in an interview that there was ''no question'' that he saw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as being directly on the ballot. That message was effective for Democrats in governor's races in several critical states last year.\par \par \uc2 ''The only reason North Carolina doesn't have a ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now is because we have a Democratic governor,'' Mr. Stein said.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Mr. Robinson declined to comment for this article.\par \par \uc2 For Democrats elsewhere, it can be more challenging to argue that their races will decide the fate of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their state, especially in places where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections are codified. And it is far too soon to know what mix of issues will ultimately determine 2024 campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Still, Democrats noted that if the Supreme Court had let the Texas ruling stand, that would have had major nationwide implications -- and many stress the possibility of national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, depending on the makeup of the White House and Congress.\par \par \uc2 ''Even though we may have current protections for this in Nevada, if a nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is imposed, Nevadans will suffer, and women will die,'' Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  of Nevada, a Democrat who recently announced her re-election bid, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Ms. Rosen called the Supreme Court order ''a temporary relief.'' But in the interview, she said the Texas ruling underscored how one conservative judge could threaten the power of a major government agency.\par \par \uc2 ''It's pretty frightening,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Top, \uc2 -rights demonstrators in the Wisconsin State Capitol in January; above left, \uc2  opponents at the March for Life in Washington, also in January. Above right, Senator Gary Peters of Michigan, who leads the Democratic Senate campaign arm. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JAMIE KELTER DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; HAIYUN JIANG/THE NEW YORK TIMES; JULIA NIKHINSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A21. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230423ej4n00069\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
