\par\par \uc2 NEWS ANALYSIS\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ruling on Pill Puts Prudence Above Power\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,232 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court's order seemed to vindicate a commitment in last year's decision in Dobbs: to leave further questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the political process.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- It was an interim ruling, and the majority gave no reasons. But the Supreme Court's order on Friday night maintaining the availability of a commonly used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill nonetheless sent a powerful message from a chastened court.\par \par \uc2 ''Legal sanity prevailed, proving that, at least for now, disrupting the national market for an F.D.A.-approved drug is a bridge too far, even for this court,'' said David S. Cohen, a law professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ten months ago, five conservative justices overturned Roe v. Wade, eliminating a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had been in place for half a century. They did so almost as soon as a third Trump appointee arrived, tilting the balance on the bench sharply to the right. All three of the Trump justices were in the majority.\par \par \uc2 Cynics might be forgiven for thinking that the decision last June, in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, was a product of raw power. The public reaction was certainly negative, as the court's approval ratings sank and the decision itself proved deeply unpopular and a political windfall for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In his concurrence in Dobbs, Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . said the majority had abandoned ''principles of judicial restraint'' at the cost of ''a serious jolt to the legal system.'' Friday's order avoided a second jolt.\par \par \uc2 But the Dobbs decision also made a kind of promise. The majority opinion, written by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 ., said at least seven times that doing away with the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an exercise of judicial modesty.\par \par \uc2 ''The authority to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be returned to the people and their elected representatives,'' Justice Alito wrote, in a formulation that, with only small variations, was sprinkled throughout the opinion like a refrain.\par \par \uc2 Friday's order, for the time being at least, vindicated that promise. The court blocked a sweeping ruling from Matthew J. Kacsmaryk, a federal judge in Texas appointed by President Donald J. Trump more noted for his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bona fides than his legal acumen.\par \par \uc2 His ruling, based on judicial second-guessing of the many scientific studies buttressing the Food and Drug Administration's approval and regulation of the pill, would have upended a status quo in place for 23 years.\par \par \uc2 Nor did the justices accept, for now, a less assertive alternative from a divided three-judge panel of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit. The majority, made up of two Trump appointees, would have sharply curtailed but not eliminated the availability of the pill.\par \par \uc2 Since the court took up the case on an expedited basis, on its so-called shadow docket, the justices could dissent without saying so publicly, making counting the votes an inexact science. On the available evidence, though, the vote on Friday night appeared to be 7 to 2.\par \par \uc2 It is all but certain that the court's three liberal members -- Justices \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  -- were in the majority. It is a very good bet that Chief Justice Roberts, who staked out a compromise position in Dobbs, was with them.\par \par \uc2 And none of the members of the court appointed by Mr. Trump -- Justices \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  -- noted a dissent.\par \par \uc2 That left two justices. One, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , voted to allow the restrictions on the pill imposed by the Fifth Circuit but gave no reasons.\par \par \uc2 The other was Justice Alito, the author of the majority opinion in Dobbs. Notwithstanding his pledges that the court was getting out of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  business, he issued a dissent that packed a lot of grievance into its roughly three pages.\par \par \uc2 That was ''very ironic and not at all surprising,'' said Greer Donley, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2  and an author, with Professor Cohen and Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, dean of Temple University Beasley School of Law, of ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Pills,'' an article to be published in The Stanford Law Review.\par \par \uc2 ''Justice Alito, who wrote so passionately about returning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states to be decided by their elected representatives, would have allowed an order to take effect that made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  less accessible only in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained legal,'' Professor Donley said.\par \par \uc2 Soon after the Biden administration and Danco Laboratories, which manufactures the pill, filed emergency applications on April 14 asking the Supreme Court to intervene, Justice Alito, who oversees the Fifth Circuit, paused Judge Kacsmaryk's ruling for five days, until Wednesday. When that deadline arrived, he paused it for a second time, until Friday.\par \par \uc2 It is not clear how the justices spent the week, as it yielded only one opinion, the dissent from Justice Alito. He devoted much of it to accusing the Biden administration of acting in bad faith.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito said, for instance, that the administration should have appealed a decision affirming \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill access from Judge Thomas O. Rice, a federal judge in Washington State appointed by President Barack Obama. Judge Rice's decision was in tension with the one from Judge Kacsmaryk, blocking the F.D.A. from limiting the availability of mifepristone in much of the country.\par \par \uc2 Leah Litman, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , said she found Justice Alito's critique curious. If there was questionable conduct, she said, it was in the Texas litigation, as the lead plaintiff, a coalition of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups known as the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, had taken steps to ensure that the case would appear before a friendly judge.\par \par \uc2 ''It was remarkable that Alito accused the federal government of bad faith in this matter for choosing not to appeal the initial order in the Washington case,'' Professor Litman said, ''when the plaintiffs in the Texas case incorporated in Amarillo so they could select Judge Kacsmaryk as the one to hear their request for a nationwide medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito added that Danco, the pill's manufacturer, would have had nothing to fear had the Supreme Court curtailed the F.D.A.'s approval of the drug while the case moved forward because, he said, the Biden administration would most likely have ignored the court's ruling.\par \par \uc2 ''The government,'' Justice Alito wrote, ''has not dispelled legitimate doubts that it would even obey an unfavorable order in these cases, much less that it would choose to take enforcement actions to which it has strong objections.''\par \par \uc2 Professor Litman said the dissent sounded more like a political argument than a legal one. ''It just generally reads like an old guy who watches a lot of Fox News and is ranting about how he had to pay for a blue check mark,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The case now returns to the Fifth Circuit, which will hear arguments on May 17. After it rules, the losing side will almost certainly appeal to the Supreme Court, and the justices would then have another chance to decide whether to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 It would be a mistake to read Friday's order as a definitive prediction of where they are headed. But there are reasons to think that an ambitious court has grown cautious.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A1, A21. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230423ej4n0005y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
