\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 After the Supreme Court Ruling: Relief, Disappointment and Vows to Keep Fighting\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani \par \uc2 892 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court kept a key \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill available while the legal fight continues.\par \par \uc2 A Supreme Court decision Friday to keep available -- for now -- the widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone was met with relief from one side of the debate, disappointment from the other and a vow from both to keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 ''This is good news, but the facts remain the same: Access to mifepristone should never have been in jeopardy in the first place,'' Planned Parenthood tweeted after the court issued a stay on two lower court rulings that would have limited access to the pills.\par \par \uc2 The court temporarily halted two separate rulings while an appeal is being considered: one from a Texas federal judge who suspended the F.D.A.'s approval of the drug, and another from an appeals court that said the pill could no longer be delivered by mail. Those rulings would have impacted the availability of the drug even in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  of Massachusetts called the court's decision a ''victory'' for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients and providers.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Healey said ''the next attack on reproductive freedom is just around the corner.'' In preparation for the possibility of the pill being pulled from the market, she last week asked the \uc2 University of Massachusetts\uc2  to purchase a one-year supply of mifepristone for the state.\par \par \uc2 ''Yes, today the status quo access to mifepristone remains,'' said Monica Simpson, executive director of SisterSong Women of Color Reproductive Justice Collective, an organization that is the lead plaintiff in a case challenging Georgia's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Ms. Simpson said ''the week-by-week uncertainty of not knowing the fate of this access'' remains despite the Friday ruling.\par \par \uc2 While statements of muted celebration poured in from elected Democrats and groups supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, comment from Republicans and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups was noticeably sparser.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Andrew Bailey of Missouri, who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said through a spokesman that the decision was a disappointment. In February, Mr. Bailey and Republican attorneys general in 21 states sent letters threatening legal action against \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS pharmacies if they dispensed mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bailey, who also filed a brief in support of the case against the F.D.A.'s approval of the drug, said he remains ''confident in the strength of our case.''\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , the conservative Christian legal advocacy organization that is representing the challengers to the F.D.A.'s approval of mifepristone, played down the ruling in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Its senior counsel, Erik Baptist, said maintaining the status quo while the lawsuit moved forward was a common practice.\par \par \uc2 ''Our case seeking to put women's health above politics continues on an expedited basis in the lower courts,'' Mr. Baptist said. ''We look forward to a final outcome in this case that will hold the F.D.A. accountable.''\par \par \uc2 Kristan Hawkins, the president of Students for Life Action, said Friday's ruling made her organization's work more urgent than ever.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, in a statement released by the White House, said his administration would continue to fight to keep mifepristone available while calling on the public to ''elect a Congress who will pass a law restoring the protections of Roe v. Wade.''\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Diana DeGette\uc2 , Democrat of Colorado, said the court had ''averted a disaster that not only would have put women's health at risk, but also threatened to upend our nation's entire drug-approval process going forward.''\par \par \uc2 ''Our fight to fully protect women's right to reproductive care is far from over,'' she wrote in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , head of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said that the Texas ruling on mifepristone access ''sowed chaos, confusion and panic,'' adding the court's decision ''is a huge relief, but we're not out of the woods yet.''\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders also used the moment to characterize Republicans as extreme. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue has been politically fraught for the G.O.P., and has been blamed for losses in the midterms as well as in a recent high-profile State Supreme Court race in Wisconsin. The party has struggled to reach consensus on policy at both the state and national levels.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  of Michigan, a Democrat who recently repealed a 1931 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in her state, said in a statement Friday, ''We need to be clear about why this case came before the court in the first place: a fringe, extreme minority that refuses to follow science or respect Americans' freedoms is judge shopping to impose their agenda on women.''\par \par \uc2 Mini Timmaraju, president of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 , said the continued availability of mifepristone was crucial, but that ''the fight, however, is far from over.''\par \par \uc2 ''Once again,'' she added, ''anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists will use the courts to push their deeply unpopular agenda and once again, we will organize the tens of millions of people who believe in safe, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight back.''\par \par \uc2 Charlie Savage and Carl Hulse contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Savage and Carl Hulse contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Maura Healey of Massachusetts said her state is stockpiling a one-year supply of mifepristone. (PHOTOGRAPH BY STEVEN SENNE/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230422ej4m0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
