\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Medical Termination of Pregnancies Was Little Known to Americans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Igielnik and Andrew Fischer \par \uc2 871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A majority of Americans had not heard of mifepristone, a survey earlier this year found. The drug is now at the center of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case headed to the Supreme Court.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have dominated headlines in the past week, but until recently, relatively few Americans were familiar with the concept of medication abortions, even as the use of pills like mifepristone has surpassed surgical procedures as the most common method to terminate pregnancies and as national support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has grown in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Medication abortions, a method typically used in the first 12 weeks of pregnancy, accounted for more than half of all abortions in the United States as of 2020, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , up from 40 percent in 2018. Two conflicting rulings on mifepristone, the first pill in a two-drug regimen used to terminate pregnancy, have put the long-term availability of the medication in limbo. The Justice Department has asked the Supreme Court to step in.\par \par \uc2 About one in three Americans said they had heard of mifepristone or a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a January survey from the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , a nonpartisan research group that focuses on health issues. But that share is most likely growing, Ashley Kirzinger, the organization's director of survey methodology, said.\par \par \uc2 ''Once the Dobbs decision came down, there was increased media attention about the accessibility across state lines,'' she said, referring to the Supreme Court decision from June that ended the federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''And that has translated into knowledge.''\par \par \uc2 Voters are not particularly supportive of state-level efforts to restrict access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Just 29 percent of Americans -- and only 38 percent of Republicans -- support these efforts, according to a Reuters/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll conducted in the days after the court rulings.\par \par \uc2 The poll also showed how the ongoing court battles related to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to reduce Americans' already-sinking confidence in courts as impartial arbiters. Only a third of Americans believed that the ruling by a federal judge in Texas to invalidate the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of mifepristone was an impartial decision, and more than half felt the decision was politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 Even many Republicans -- about 50 percent -- saw the ruling as a political one.\par \par \uc2 And there is already wide distrust of the Supreme Court and dissatisfaction with its past decisions.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 New York Times\uc2 /Siena College poll last July, shortly after the Dobbs decision, found that a majority of Americans held an unfavorable view of the Supreme Court, and more than 60 percent of voters, including 39 percent of Republicans, viewed the Supreme Court's decisions as politically motivated. Overall, 61 percent of voters disagreed with the Dobbs decision, including 30 percent of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Nearly half of Americans were unsure if medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal in their state, according to the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  survey, which was conducted before the court rulings on mifepristone raised the profile of the drug.\par \par \uc2 There was particular confusion among respondents in states where abortions were illegal: 10 percent of women living in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned incorrectly believed medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal in their state, and another 50 percent were unsure.\par \par \uc2 Views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tend to change little over time and are mostly divided along partisan lines. About 60 percent of Americans support some form of legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with about 80 percent of Americans who are Democrats or say they lean toward Democrats supporting legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all or most cases, and about 40 percent of Republicans or Republican-leaning Americans supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, according to \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 . Support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has increased slightly in recent years, largely among Democrats, according to \uc2 Pew\uc2 's data.\par \par \uc2 Views on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also differ by party, but there's not a clear majority of Republicans who say it should be illegal. About a third of Republicans do hold that view, according to a poll by \uc2 Pew\uc2  taken before the rulings, while roughly a third thought medication abortions should be legal and the remaining 29 percent weren't sure. About half of all Americans, and more than 70 percent of Democrats, supported legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 ''As we have long seen with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  overall, partisanship is the driver of differences in views more so than gender,'' said Hannah Hartig, a researcher at Pew who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''We do not see much difference, for example, between Republican men and women in their views on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 There are wide differences within parties, however, based on the particular circumstances of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Reuters/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll found Republicans would be significantly more likely to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication if the pills were obtained at a doctor's office, clinic or pharmacy as opposed to mailed. And significantly more Americans -- across the partisan spectrum -- are more supportive of a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at 16 weeks of gestation than a national ban at six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is the first pill in a common drug regimen used to end pregnancies medically. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GABRIELA BHASKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230415ej4f0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
