\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 How Possible Rivals in 2024 View  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maggie Astor \par \uc2 1,017 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Not quite a year after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to be one of the main issues shaping American politics.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not fading as a driving issue in America, coming up again and again everywhere policy is decided: in legislatures, courts, the Oval Office and voting booths.\par \par \uc2 An 11-point liberal victory in a pivotal Wisconsin Supreme Court race last week was fueled by the issue. Days later, a Texas judge invalidated the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's 23-year-old approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone (late Wednesday, an appeals court partly stayed the ruling but imposed some restrictions). And Florida, under Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a likely Republican presidential candidate, is poised to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks' gestation.\par \par \uc2 The fallout from the Supreme Court's revocation of a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year looks poised to be a major issue in the upcoming presidential race. So where do the likely candidates stand?\par \par \uc2 Here is what some of the most prominent contenders, declared and likely, have said and done:\par \par \uc2 President Biden\par \par \uc2 President Biden condemned the ruling invalidating the approval of mifepristone, which his administration is appealing, and called it ''another unprecedented step in taking away basic freedoms from women and putting their health at risk.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has a complicated history with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; before his 2020 presidential campaign, he supported restrictions, including the Hyde Amendment, which prohibits federal funding for most abortions. But he has since spoken more forcefully in defense of unfettered access, including endorsing congressional codification of the rights Roe v. Wade used to protect.\par \par \uc2 White House officials have said he is not willing to disregard the mifepristone ruling, as some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists have urged.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has said he is planning to run in 2024, but has not formally declared his candidacy.\par \par \uc2 Donald J. Trump\par \par \uc2 More than perhaps any other Republican, former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  is responsible for the current state of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access: He appointed three of the six Supreme Court justices who overturned Roe v. Wade and the district judge who invalidated the approval of mifepristone. But lately, he has been loath to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Mr. Trump privately expressed concern that the ruling overturning Roe would hurt Republicans -- and it did, both in the midterms and in the Wisconsin Supreme Court election.\par \par \uc2 If elected again, he would be under tremendous pressure from the social conservatives who have fueled the Republican Party for decades -- and who helped elect him in 2016 -- to support a national ban. He has not said whether he would do so.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, whom polls show as the top potential Republican competitor to Mr. Trump, is pushing forward with the Florida Legislature to ban most abortions after six weeks. The bill passed on Thursday and was sent to Mr. DeSantis's desk. Polls show that most Americans, including Floridians, oppose six-week bans.\par \par \uc2 It is a more aggressive posture than he took last year, when Florida enacted a ban after 15 weeks and Mr. DeSantis -- facing re-election in November -- did not commit to going further. He made his move after winning re-election by a sweeping margin.\par \par \uc2 Nikki Haley\par \par \uc2 At a campaign event in Iowa this week, Nikki Haley, a former governor of South Carolina and former United Nations ambassador, gestured away from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  absolutism -- saying that she did not ''want unelected judges deciding something this personal.''\par \par \uc2 But her comments were muddled: She said she wanted to leave the issue to the states, but at the same time suggested that she would be open to a federal ban if she thought there was momentum for one.\par \par \uc2 ''This is about saving as many babies as we can,'' she said, while adding that she did not want to play the ''game'' of specifying when in pregnancy she believed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be allowed.\par \par \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\par \par \uc2 Since starting his presidential campaign this month, former Gov. Asa Hutchinson of Arkansas has said only that he is ''proud to stand squarely on my pro-life position'' when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He has not detailed what, if any, federal legislation he would support.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Mr. Hutchinson criticized the lack of an exception for rape and incest in an Arkansas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban he had signed. When he signed it, he said that he wanted the exception but legislators didn't, and that he accepted their judgment as the will of voters -- though a poll last year found that more than 70 percent of Arkansans supported such an exception.\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\par \par \uc2 A staunch social conservative, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has been more open than most Republicans about continuing to advertise his opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Life won again today,'' he said in a statement on the mifepristone ruling. ''When it approved chemical abortions on demand, the F.D.A. acted carelessly and with blatant disregard for human life.''\par \par \uc2 Last year, Mr. Pence said anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists ''must not rest'' until \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was outlawed nationwide. Mr. Pence is considering a 2024 run, but has not formally joined the race.\par \par \uc2 Tim Scott\par \par \uc2 Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina repeatedly dodged questions about whether he supported federal restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the days after announcing a presidential exploratory committee this week.\par \par \uc2 Asked in an interview with CBS News whether he supported a 15-week ban, he called himself ''100 percent pro-life.'' When the interviewer suggested that his stance indicated he would support a 15-week ban, he replied, ''That's not what I said.''\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, he told WMUR, a New Hampshire news station, that he would support a 20-week ban, but still did not say whether he would back something stricter.\par \par \uc2 ''This is about saving as many babies as we can,'' Nikki Haley said during a campaign event. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HAIYUN JIANG/THE NEW YORK TIMES); Asa Hutchinson said that he was ''proud to stand squarely on my pro-life position.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY SCOTT MORGAN/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230414ej4e0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
