\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 2 New Rulings Muddy Waters Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,737 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The justices are poised to consider whether the most common method of ending pregnancies can be sharply curtailed in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal.\par \par \uc2 Less than a year after the Supreme Court declared it was ceding the matter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to elected officials, the justices are poised to consider whether the most common method of ending pregnancies can be sharply curtailed in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal, not just where it is illegal.\par \par \uc2 After a federal appeals court imposed several barriers to access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill late Wednesday night, the Justice Department announced on Thursday that it would seek emergency relief from the justices, asking them to block the ruling while a fast-tracked appeal moved forward.\par \par \uc2 The appellate ruling, from a divided three-judge panel of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit in New Orleans, said the pill, mifepristone, could remain available while the lawsuit, filed against the Food and Drug Administration by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, proceeded through the courts.\par \par \uc2 In its order, the panel partly rejected a ruling from Judge Matthew J. Kacsmaryk of the Northern District of Texas, who declared last week that the F.D.A.'s approval of mifepristone in 2000 was not valid, in essence saying that the drug should be pulled from the market.\par \par \uc2 But the panel blocked a series of steps the F.D.A. took in recent years to ease access to the drug -- including allowing it to be sent through the mail and prescribed by health care providers who are not doctors.\par \par \uc2 The appellate court said its ruling would hold until the full case was heard on its merits.\par \par \uc2 But Thursday afternoon, a federal judge in another mifepristone lawsuit issued an order that required the F.D.A. not to limit access to the drug in much of the country.\par \par \uc2 That lawsuit, filed by Democratic attorneys general in 17 states and the District of Columbia, challenged extra restrictions the F.D.A. imposes on mifepristone. Judge Thomas O. Rice of the U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Washington, an Obama appointee, last Friday blocked the agency from curbing the availability of mifepristone in those states.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, he reaffirmed that order said that it ''must be followed'' by the F.D.A. ''irrespective'' of what the Fifth Circuit appeals court would do.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts said the dueling federal court orders could make it more likely that the Supreme Court will need to resolve the status of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill.\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court eliminated the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, overturning a half-century of precedents, it made a vow. Writing for the majority in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . said that ''the authority to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be returned to the people and their elected representatives.''\par \par \uc2 ''Women on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue,'' he wrote, should ''seek to affect the legislative process by influencing public opinion, lobbying legislators, voting and running for office.'' The early evidence suggests that supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are winning those political battles.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, the new case, taking issue with an administrative agency's scientific assessment of the safety of the pill, indicates that opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to think that the judiciary is their most potent ally.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs decision concerned the Constitution, while the appeals court's ruling mostly turned on principles of administrative law. But the new case demonstrates that legal disputes over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to engage the justices.\par \par \uc2 They will most likely rule on the Biden administration's emergency application in a matter of days and may restore full access to the pill, endorse the restrictions outlined by the appeals court or suspend access entirely, as a federal judge in Texas did. The appeal will then proceed and probably return to the justices for a ruling on the merits, further embroiling them in a fight they said they had abandoned.\par \par \uc2 The case has attracted interest beyond the groups that usually weigh in on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases. Hundreds of pharmaceutical industry leaders and investors issued a scathing condemnation of the ruling invalidating mifepristone's approval and filed a brief supporting the F.D.A. in the case.\par \par \uc2 ''If courts can overturn drug approvals without regard for science or evidence, or for the complexity required to fully vet the safety and efficacy of new drugs, any medicine is at risk for the same outcome as mifepristone,'' they wrote in a statement on Monday. Leaving the fate of medicines in the hands of jurists, they argued, would have a chilling effect on drug development in the United States, reducing incentives for investment and innovation.\par \par \uc2 In its order, the appellate panel said the F.D.A.'s approval of mifepristone could stand because too much time had passed for the plaintiffs, a consortium of groups and doctors opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to challenge that decision. The court also seemed to take into account the government's view that removing a long-approved drug from the market would have ''significant public consequences.''\par \par \uc2 But the appellate court said that it was not too late for the plaintiffs to challenge a set of steps the F.D.A. took, beginning in 2016, that lifted restrictions and made it easier for more patients to have access to the pill.\par \par \uc2 The court also said that the government could not logically claim that the changes made since 2016 were crucial to the public, ''given that the nation operated -- and mifepristone was administered to millions of women -- without them for 16 years'' after the initial pill approval.\par \par \uc2 Such steps significantly expanded access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is now used in more than half of pregnancy terminations in the United States. It usually involves taking mifepristone -- which blocks a hormone that allows a pregnancy to develop -- followed one or two days later by another drug, misoprostol, which causes contractions similar to a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 The ruling would reinstate measures that required mifepristone to be prescribed and dispensed only by a doctor and picked up in person by the patient, who would have to visit the doctor three times during the medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  process.\par \par \uc2 Changes since 2016 also included the F.D.A.'s approval in 2019 of a generic version of mifepristone, manufactured by GenBioPro, whose product is now used by many telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and clinics.\par \par \uc2 Evan Masingill, the chief executive of GenBioPro, said in a statement on Thursday: ''In the United States, once a drug has been through the rigorous F.D.A. review process and received approval, federal law protects the right to market the drug. GenBioPro will use all regulatory and legal tools to protect access to mifepristone for patients and providers.''\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for the plaintiffs, Erin Hawley, senior counsel for the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , a conservative Christian legal organization, said at a news media briefing on Thursday that ''the Fifth Circuit's decision is a significant victory for the doctors we represent, women's health and every American who deserves an accountable federal government acting within the bounds of the law.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Kacsmaryk, a Trump appointee who has written critically of the Roe v. Wade decision, had stayed his order for seven days to give the F.D.A. time to appeal. On Monday, the F.D.A. asked the appeals court to extend that stay, and the judges partly granted that request.\par \par \uc2 In the decision, which came just before midnight on Wednesday, two Trump-appointed judges voted to reimpose some of the restrictions that the F.D.A. had eased. The third judge, appointed by President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , said she would essentially have granted the full request. All of those restrictions were temporarily reinstated.\par \par \uc2 The 42-page appeals court opinion appeared to accept several of the claims of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  plaintiffs and used some of the terminology of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, referring to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as ''chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 '' and in one instance referring to a fetus or embryo as ''an unborn child.''\par \par \uc2 In their lawsuit, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents claim that mifepristone is unsafe, causing ''cramping, heavy bleeding and severe pain,'' and that the F.D.A. has ignored safety risks and never adequately evaluated the scientific evidence.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. vigorously disputes this claim, as do mainstream medical organizations. They say that bleeding and cramping are normal consequences of the process, a sign that the pregnancy tissue is being expelled, and cite years of scientific studies that show that serious complications are rare, resulting in less than 1 percent of patients needing hospitalization. The F.D.A. applies a special regulatory framework to mifepristone, meaning that it has been regulated much more strictly and studied more intensively than most other drugs.\par \par \uc2 In seeking a stay of Judge Kacsmaryk's ruling, lawyers from the Justice Department, representing the F.D.A., wrote, ''There is no basis in science or fact for plaintiffs' repeated claims that mifepristone is unsafe when used in the manner approved by F.D.A.''\par \par \uc2 The appeals court did not evaluate all of the safety arguments in the case, but it said that the F.D.A. ''cannot deny that serious complications from mifepristone'' occur and said that the agreement form that the agency requires patients to sign stipulates that the drug can carry risks. The court also said that the F.D.A. was incorrect in saying that mifepristone was comparable in safety to ibuprofen. ''F.D.A.'s own documents show that mifepristone bears no resemblance to ibuprofen,'' the court said.\par \par \uc2 The appeals court also seemed to agree with the plaintiffs that a 19th-century law called the Comstock Act prevents the mailing of drugs used for abortions. The Justice Department said in a recent memo that the act prohibits mailing the pills only if the sender knows they will be used for an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not if the patient is in a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 The appeals court wrote that ''merely by knowingly making use of the mail for a prohibited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  item'' would violate that law.\par \par \uc2 Mike Ives contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Mike Ives contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 An appeals court ruling would reinstate requirements that mifepristone be dispensed only by a doctor, instead of by mail. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ERIN SCHAFF/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14) This article appeared in print on page A1, A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230414ej4e0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
