\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democratic States Move to Stockpile  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills After Texas Ruling on F.D.A.\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen and Ava Sasani \par \uc2 1,205 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal judge's decision to invalidate the F.D.A.'s approval of mifepristone could affect availability even in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 The governor of Massachusetts has asked the \uc2 University of Massachusetts\uc2  to purchase a one-year supply of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill mifepristone, and issued an executive order shielding pharmacists who stock the drug, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and patients from criminal and civil liability.\par \par \uc2 Washington State is using its Department of Corrections and the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2  to stockpile a four-year supply of the drug, and has fast-tracked legislation allowing health care providers to distribute it.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Maine, where at least 70 percent of abortions are done using pills, and in Maryland said this week that they were exploring purchasing additional doses of mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Since a federal judge in Texas last week invalidated the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's 23-year-old approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone, states led by Democrats have been scrambling to adjust to a possible future without it.\par \par \uc2 The judge's decision could affect access to the pill in all states, even where Democrats and voters have acted to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The pill is the first in a two-drug medication regimen used in over half of abortions in the United States. A second pill, misoprostol, can be used alone but is not as effective as both drugs together.\par \par \uc2 The judge, Matthew J. Kacsmaryk, put his ruling on hold for a week to allow time for appeals; the Justice Department on Monday asked the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit to pause the ruling while the appeals process moves forward. A separate ruling by a Washington State federal judge hours after the Texas ruling directly contradicted the Texas judge and ordered the F.D.A. to make no changes to the availability of the drug in the states involved in that suit. Many expect the dispute to head to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 In the interim, states are trying to figure out how to prepare for different scenarios in a fast-changing legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 Some states are looking to misoprostol, the second drug in the two-drug regimen, which was not covered in the Texas judge's preliminary ruling.\par \par \uc2 In California, Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  announced on Monday that the state had purchased an ''emergency stockpile'' of 250,000 pills of misoprostol and negotiated the purchase of up to two million.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Newsom opted to focus on misoprostol instead of mifepristone so that ''people across California and the country know medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain accessible and affordable -- regardless of what happens in the court -- without disruption,'' said Brandon Richards, a spokesman for the governor.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York announced that the state Health Department would immediately begin purchasing 150,000 doses of misoprostol, or a five-year supply.\par \par \uc2 If the Texas ruling stands, Ms. Hochul said that New York would dedicate up to $20 million more for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on top of the $35 million that has already been dedicated. She also said she was in talks with legislators about requiring private insurance to cover medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it is prescribed off label.\par \par \uc2 ''We'll always protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and all individual rights here in New York,'' she said during a Planned Parenthood of Greater New York news conference.\par \par \uc2 Some in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights worry that moves to stock up on misoprostol in lieu of mifepristone is a hedge that could have unintended consequences. Mary Ziegler, a law professor and historian of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , said such decisions might unintentionally signal that the mifepristone should not be used in medication abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''I think they're looking at this and thinking, 'I don't know who is going to be in the White House in 2024, we can't rely on the F.D.A.,''' she said of states considering the option. ''It's premature to do that and it might frighten people unnecessarily. We're in a climate where a lot of people don't know what the law is.''\par \par \uc2 The legal confusion has also made some states hesitate in making concrete plans, though some Democratic leaders said they expected more states to focus on securing access to pills.\par \par \uc2 ''I am confident, too, in speaking with other governors around the country, that you will see more and more states step up,'' said Gov. \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  of Massachusetts at a news conference on Monday, with Senator Elizabeth Warren and others by her side. ''The battle to ban abortions has been fought in the states. The battle to win reproductive freedom and women's right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be won in the states, and that's what you see happening here.''\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Ms. Healey said that the state had been discussing, for several weeks, how to structure a purchase order and who the appropriate purchasing agent would be.\par \par \uc2 In order to purchase and distribute the drug, Washington state needed to use a state-run agency with a pharmacy license, said Gov. \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  in an interview last week.\par \par \uc2 Jaime Smith, a spokeswoman for Mr. Inslee, said the sale and distribution of mifepristone in Washington would not be affected by the Texas case.\par \par \uc2 ''Our state law regarding distribution of prescribed medication does not require F.D.A. approval,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Some states, like Pennsylvania, have been publicizing information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication still being legal and available. In Connecticut, \uc2 William Tong\uc2 , the attorney general, said he had been talking to pharmacy chains to remind them the drug was still legal in their state.\par \par \uc2 In Illinois, \uc2 Don Harmon\uc2 , the Senate president, called the ruling in Texas ''disappointing but not at all surprising'' and pointed to legislation protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that was signed into law in January.\par \par \uc2 ''We saw this coming in Illinois and enacted legislation protecting access to a wide suite of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care so that women can continue to make their own decisions about their bodies,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Illinois's governor, JB Pritzker, signed a law protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and out-of-state patients from prosecution. The law also requires health insurers to provide coverage of medication abortions.\par \par \uc2 ''As the legal landscape changes, we will be doing everything possible to make sure that mifepristone is available in Illinois and covered by insurance,'' said Olivia Kuncio, a spokeswoman for the governor. ''However, no matter the results of that ruling, misoprostol -- not the gold standard for medication abortions, but still an option -- will remain an option for medication abortions, and insurance and Medicaid will continue to cover that drug.''\par \par \uc2 This week, a group of local governments joined an amicus brief filed by the Public Rights Project, a civil rights nonprofit, in support of the Biden administration's request for an emergency stay of Judge Kacsmaryk's ruling. The parties include Houston, Cincinnati, Tucson, Milwaukee County, and Bucks County in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Audio produced by Parin Behrooz.\par \par \uc2 Lola Fadulu contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Audio produced by Parin Behrooz. Lola Fadulu contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230413ej4d0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
