\par\par \uc2 NEWS ANALYSIS\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 For Conservative Justices, Upholding  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill Ruling Is No Sure Thing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,174 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Even justices hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the administrative state may think twice, legal scholars said, before embracing a Texas judge's decision.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The conservative legal movement has long had two key goals: to limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to restrict the authority of administrative agencies.\par \par \uc2 The decision last week by a federal judge in Texas invalidating the Food and Drug Administration's approval 23 years ago of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone checked both of those boxes. The ruling, if it stands, would not only thwart access to the pills, used in more than half of pregnancy terminations, but also undermine the F.D.A.'s authority to approve and regulate other drugs.\par \par \uc2 At first blush, all of that might seem to make the decision's chances of surviving review by a Supreme Court dominated by conservative justices quite promising.\par \par \uc2 But legal scholars said on Monday that the poor quality, breathtaking sweep and unknown collateral consequences of the Texas decision might cause at least some of the Supreme Court's conservative justices to wait for a case that would allow them to take more measured steps.\par \par \uc2 ''If you're a justice looking for a case in which to undermine the administrative state, this is not a particularly elegant one,'' said Mary Ziegler, a law professor and historian at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 . ''Everything about this case makes it an imperfect vehicle, except for the fact that it's about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the administrative state. This is boundary testing.''\par \par \uc2 Jonathan H. Adler, a law professor at \uc2 Case Western Reserve University\uc2 , said the new case, should it reach the Supreme Court, might meet a reception similar to that of the latest challenge to the Affordable Care Act. In 2021, by a vote of 7 to 2, the court said that the 18 Republican-led states and two individuals who brought the case had not suffered the sort of direct injury that gave them standing to sue.\par \par \uc2 Despite the conservative majority's misgivings about the health care law, Professor Adler said, ''when push came to shove and they were presented with a fundamentally deficient legal theory, only two justices were willing to give that legal theory the time of day.''\par \par \uc2 History may repeat itself in the Texas case, he said. ''I view some of the administrative law aspects of this case to be similar,'' he said, noting that there were significant threshold issues involving the plaintiffs' standing to sue, whether they had exhausted other avenues for relief and whether they had taken too long to bring an action.\par \par \uc2 ''Unless the court is willing to create \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -specific rules for administrative law, it has to reject these claims,'' Professor Adler said.\par \par \uc2 For decades, conservative justices complained that the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  jurisprudence was destabilizing the rule of law, warping principles that applied in all sorts of cases.\par \par \uc2 ''No legal rule or doctrine is safe from ad hoc nullification by this court when an occasion for its application arises in a case involving state regulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Justice \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2  wrote in 1986.\par \par \uc2 The court's decision in June in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, which overruled Roe v. Wade and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, promised to put an end to such collateral damage.\par \par \uc2 ''Roe and Casey have led to the distortion of many important but unrelated legal doctrines, and that effect provides further support for overruling those decisions,'' Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . wrote for the majority\par \par \uc2 But a ruling upholding the Texas decision could lead to distortions of just the sort that Dobbs had vowed to end, Professor Ziegler said. ''There's a fun-house mirror effect to all of this,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The court could also leave itself open to charges of inconsistency and opportunism.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, even in the context of access to mifepristone, the Supreme Court has recently affirmed the authority of the F.D.A. In 2021, the court reinstated a requirement that women seeking to end their pregnancies using the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill pick it up in person from a hospital or medical office.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Maryland had blocked the requirement in light of the coronavirus pandemic, overriding the agency's determination. Chief Justice Roberts, the only member of the majority to set out his reasons, said that the trial judge should have deferred to the F.D.A.\par \par \uc2 ''My view is that courts owe significant deference to the politically accountable entities with the 'background, competence and expertise to assess public health,''' he wrote, quoting an earlier opinion. ''In light of those considerations, I do not see a sufficient basis here for the district court to compel the F.D.A. to alter the regimen for medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 In 2020, in an earlier encounter with the case, Justice Alito, joined by Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , expressed incredulity that ''a district court judge in Maryland took it upon himself to overrule the F.D.A. on a question of drug safety.'' (The Biden administration later removed the requirement that the pill be dispensed in person.)\par \par \uc2 In a dissent in 2009, Justice Alito, joined by Chief Justice Roberts and Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , praised the agency's expertise, saying ''the F.D.A. has the benefit of the long view.''\par \par \uc2 The majority had ruled that the agency's approval of a drug had not displaced injured plaintiffs' ability to sue under state law. Justice Alito disagreed. ''Where the F.D.A. determines, in accordance with its statutory mandate, that a drug is on balance 'safe,''' he wrote, the court's precedents ''prohibit any state from countermanding that determination.''\par \par \uc2 Rachel Rebouch\u233\'00\'E9, dean of Temple University Beasley School of Law, said the outcome of the case from Texas, if it reaches the Supreme Court, could turn on Chief Justice Roberts and Justice \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2 , who often hold the pivotal votes in close cases.\par \par \uc2 ''Both of them expressed skepticism in Dobbs about just how far any decision should go,'' she said. ''To have federal court power used in service of undercutting the ability of an expert agency to apply its congressional authority might be too much.''\par \par \uc2 Professor Ziegler agreed that Justice Kavanaugh may well be in play.\par \par \uc2 ''No matter how hostile he is to the administrative state and how hostile he is to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights,'' she said, ''there are procedural hurdles that give someone like him an out if he just doesn't want egg on his face after proclaiming that the court is getting out of the business of ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''There are reasons for justices unsympathetic to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and the administrative decision to pause here. That doesn't mean they will.''\par \par \uc2 Boxes of mifepristone, the first pill to be administered in a medical \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS); A Supreme Court ruling upholding the Texas decision could distort unrelated legal doctrines. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HAIYUN JIANG/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230411ej4b0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
