\par\par \uc2 Graphics; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Are  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills Safe? Here's the Evidence.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Schoenfeld Walker, Jonathan Corum, Malika Khurana and Ashley Wu \par \uc2 1,400 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers often say that the pills are safer than many common drugs, such as Tylenol and Viagra. Drug safety experts do not typically compare drugs in this way, and they instead assess the safety of a given medication against other choices.\par \par \uc2 For pregnant women considering medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the alternatives would be childbirth or procedural \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''There may be a political fight here, but there's not a lot of scientific ambiguity about the safety and effectiveness of this product,'' said Dr. Caleb Alexander, a professor of epidemiology and medicine at the \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2 , and a co-director of the Center for Drug Safety and Effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 Despite the extensive scientific record on the safety of the two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in the United States have asked a federal judge to block the drugs from being used to terminate pregnancies, arguing that they are dangerous.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the judge issued a preliminary ruling in that case, signaling he intends to invalidate the \uc2 U.S. Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of mifepristone. Soon after, a different judge issued a contradictory ruling saying the F.D.A. should not restrict the pill's availability. Resolving the dispute will most likely fall to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 New limits on mifepristone could upend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the country, where more than half of abortions are done with medication. The drugs are typically used through the first 12 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''The use of these two chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs causes significant injuries and harms to pregnant women and girls,'' the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups state in their complaint.\par \par \uc2 As evidence of this harm, the plaintiffs cite a handful of studies, none of which contradict the body of research in the Times review. Instead, the cited studies point to patient experiences that are common and expected, such as bleeding and pain, or experiences that are not a clear measure of serious complications, such as visits to the emergency room after taking the pills.\par \par \uc2 Almost all patients will experience bleeding and pain during a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because the pills essentially trigger a miscarriage.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not a pleasant experience. The bleeding does naturally make people worry whether it is a normal amount or if the pregnancy tissue has passed,'' said Ushma Upadhyay, a professor of reproductive sciences at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some patients who become concerned about the amount of bleeding or other side effects will go to the emergency room after taking the pills, but Dr. Upadhyay's research shows that most do not face serious complications.\par \par \uc2 And while the pills are about 95 percent effective, about 3 to 5 percent of patients need an additional procedure to remove remaining tissue or terminate the pregnancy. Providers say these are not typically dangerous situations.\par \par \uc2 ''These are no more risky than if you had a surgical procedure from the beginning,'' said Dr. Adam R. Jacobs, the medical director of complex family planning at \uc2 Mount Sinai Hospital\uc2  in New York. ''Still very safe, with a low risk for complications.''\par \par \uc2 In the lawsuit against the F.D.A. that seeks to block the use of the pills, the plaintiffs relied on five studies to argue that the pills cause harm: three from researchers in Finland and two from the Charlotte Lozier Institute, an arm of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America.\par \par \uc2 Only one of the studies cited by the plaintiffs reports on serious complications of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill regimen within the 12-week window typically used in the United States. (Two others looked at safety further along in pregnancy.)\par \par \uc2 That study reviewed records for more than 40,000 medication abortions conducted in Finland in the early 2000s. It reported that 20 percent of patients followed up with a clinician, most often for bleeding and less commonly for a procedure to evacuate the uterus and complete the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs and other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups say the 20 percent figure from this single study suggests that patients have a high risk of complications after taking the pills. But the study itself notes that bleeding is expected, serious complications are rare and medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is safe.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oskari Heikinheimo, an author of the Finland study and a professor of obstetrics and gynecology at the University of Helsinki, said in an interview that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are misinterpreting the data.\par \par \uc2 ''The correct scientific way would be to look at the whole body of evidence,'' Dr. Heikinheimo said. ''If you just pick the results that you personally like, then that's pure nonsense.''\par \par \uc2 In response, Dr. Donna Harrison, the chair of the board of the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, the plaintiff in the lawsuit, said that other researchers are calling moderate what she thinks are serious complications.\par \par \uc2 ''I agree with him that you can't pick and choose the results that you want,'' she said. ''You have to look at what this means for women.''\par \par \uc2 One of the most important measures of safety for any medication is the risk of death. Across the 101 studies and 124,000 medication abortions covered in the Times review, researchers reported one death from infection related to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and one death from unrelated causes.\par \par \uc2 The F.D.A. reported that during the period since it approved the regimen in 2000 through June last year, approximately 5.6 million women in the United States took the pills and 28 have died, or 0.0005 percent. The agency reported that some of those deaths may have been because of other causes.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reviewed 147 published research articles that examined the safety and effectiveness of mifepristone and misoprostol during the first trimester of pregnancy. The papers were cited in five systematic reviews of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and were published from 1991 to 2023. The Times also included research studies used by the F.D.A. in its medical reviews of the drugs, and those cited by the plaintiffs and defendants in Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine v. \uc2 U.S. Food and Drug Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many studies reported the number of patients experiencing ''serious adverse events,'' which generally included blood transfusions, hospitalization, major surgery or serious infection. Emergency department or clinical visits without one of these treatments, or routine but uncommon follow-up procedures to complete an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , were typically reported as adverse events, but not serious ones. The Times used these counts of serious adverse events to determine the percentage of patients in each study who did not experience serious complications.\par \par \uc2 The Times excluded 46 of the studies from the analysis, either because they reported common side effects but did not clearly state whether any serious adverse events had occurred, or because their reported data did not allow for an accurate count. None of the excluded studies concluded that the pills are unsafe.\par \par \uc2 For studies that reported detailed outcomes without noting which events were serious, The Times counted any reported blood transfusions, hospitalizations, major surgeries, serious infections or deaths as serious adverse events. The Times also discounted any serious complications like heart disease or dengue fever that were reported as unrelated to the medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Duplicate counts of patients facing more than one serious complication were removed whenever possible, however, double-counting may have slightly reduced the calculated safety rate for some studies.\par \par \uc2 The Times asked several researchers for feedback on this analysis, including drug safety experts and authors of systematic reviews of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  safety. These include: Dr. Caleb Alexander, professor of epidemiology and medicine at the \uc2 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health\uc2  and co-director of the Center for Drug Safety and Effectiveness; Dr. Melissa J. Chen, associate professor of obstetrics and gynecology at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 ; Dr. Oskari Heikinheimo, professor of obstetrics and gynecology at the University of Helsinki; Leah Koenig, data analyst, Advancing New Standards in \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 ; Dr. Nathalie Kapp, chief medical officer, \uc2 International Planned Parenthood Federation\uc2 ; Sarah Raifman, project director, Advancing New Standards in \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 ; Dr. Maria I. Rodriguez, professor of obstetrics and gynecology at \uc2 Oregon Health & Science University\uc2 ; Ushma Upadhyay, professor with Advancing New Standards in \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Additional production by Keith Collins. Monica Hersher and Sarah Cahalan contributed research.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230410ej4a0004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
