\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Idaho Targets Minors Going Out of State For Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen \par \uc2 725 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The first-of-its-kind law imposes criminal penalties on anyone who helps a person under 18 leave the state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and classifies it as ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking.''\par \par \uc2 Idaho on Wednesday became the first state to make it illegal for minors to leave the state to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without parental consent.\par \par \uc2 The new law, signed by Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 , would create a crime called ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trafficking,'' carrying a penalty of two to five years in jail for anyone who helps a person under 18 get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills without permission from a parent or guardian.\par \par \uc2 The law allows the filing of lawsuits against doctors who perform such abortions, even if the doctors live outside the state. And if local prosecutors refuse to enforce the new law, the Idaho attorney general will have ''sole discretion'' to do so.\par \par \uc2 As one of 13 states that now ban most abortions, Idaho already imposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations that are among the strictest in the country. The state bans most abortions, with narrow exceptions to preserve the life of the mother or in cases of rape or incest. It also allows family members to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in civil court.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in some neighboring states, including Washington and Oregon.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's Supreme Court also ruled in January that the State Constitution does not include a right to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Still, sponsors of the new law, which sailed through the Legislature, praised the additional measures as crucial to protect children and parental rights.\par \par \uc2 Critics of the new law said it overstepped state authority by punishing out-of-state travel.\par \par \uc2 In a letter Wednesday formalizing the law's enactment, Governor Little, a Republican, hailed the rights of states to set \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision last year in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, which overturned the nationally established right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He also took issue with characterizations that the law would ''criminalize, preclude or otherwise impair interstate travel.'' Instead, he wrote, the bill ''seeks only to prevent unemancipated minor girls from being taken across state lines for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the knowledge and consent of her parent or guardian.''\par \par \uc2 The law is scheduled to take effect in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 Mistie DelliCarpini-Tolman, the Idaho state director of Planned Parenthood Alliance Advocates, called the measure ''despicable.''\par \par \uc2 ''This law should serve as a warning to everyone living in states with lawmakers that are hostile to this critical health care procedure: This could be coming to your state, too,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Among those who helped draft the new law was Megan Wold, a lobbyist for Right to Life Idaho and a former law clerk to Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wold introduced the bill in a House committee, saying that pregnant girls were vulnerable to men who wanted to arrange a ''secret \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are expected to challenge the law in court. Some have noted that Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , who concurred with the Supreme Court's decision on Dobbs, suggested that a state could not block people from traveling outside their home states to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ''based on their constitutional right to interstate travel.''\par \par \uc2 A bill in Washington that would provide protection from subpoenas or court orders for those who travel to Washington from other states for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care has passed the State House and is now in a Senate committee.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  of Washington, a Democrat, sent Mr. Little a letter on Tuesday, urging him not to sign a bill with ''harmful impacts.'' He added, ''We will protect our providers, and we will harbor and comfort your residents who seek health care services that are denied to them in Idaho.''\par \par \uc2 Some Idaho doctors have expressed concerns as well. Last month, a hospital in northern Idaho, citing the political climate, announced that it would no longer provide labor and delivery services because doctors were departing.\par \par \uc2 ''The Idaho Legislature continues to introduce and pass bills that criminalize physicians for medical care nationally recognized as the standard of care,'' the hospital said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 A new law by Brad Little, governor of Idaho, requires parental consent for abortions. This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230407ej470002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
