\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats Run on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 , Even for Offices Without a Say\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reid J. Epstein \par \uc2 1,643 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 April 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GREEN BAY, Wis. -- Eric Genrich is running a full-throated campaign in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, reminding voters of his position at every turn and hammering his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponent in television ads. At a recent event, he featured an obstetrician who now commutes to a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal to treat patients and a local woman who traveled to Colorado to terminate a nonviable pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 There's just one inconvenient reality: Mr. Genrich is running for re-election as mayor of Green Bay, Wis., an office that has nothing to do with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.\par \par \uc2 Even before the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last summer, putting back into effect a Wisconsin law from 1849 that bans nearly all abortions, the city did not have a clinic that performed the procedure, nor a health department that regulated it.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Genrich is one of several candidates for municipal offices on the ballot this spring in races in Wisconsin, Chicago, St. Louis, Lincoln, Neb., and elsewhere who are making their support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights -- and often their opponent's past opposition -- a centerpiece of their campaigns, even though \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in all of these places is decided at the state level.\par \par \uc2 Democrats used a muscular defense of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to great success in the midterm elections last fall, and, if that strategy works again, they are likely to copy it next year in races at all levels of government, including in President Biden's campaign if he seeks re-election.\par \par \uc2 The focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in down-ballot races, however, reflects Democrats' increased nationalization of local politics. For decades, local Republican candidates ran on issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , immigration and national security, putting them in simple terms: ''A noun, a verb and 9/11,'' Mr. Biden once said in describing the phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 Now Democrats are doing the same on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in left-leaning cities, hoping to win over independent voters and some moderate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Doing so allows Democrats to avoid discussing crime rates or other less appealing campaign topics. But beyond that, they recognize and emphasize that in today's tribal politics, the precise responsibilities of an office matter less than sending a strong signal to voters about one's broader political loyalties.\par \par \uc2 ''It's definitely not a municipal issue per se,'' Mr. Genrich said in an interview. ''Voters don't care about some of these parochial distinctions between municipal boundaries. This is a city issue, a state issue, a federal issue. Some of their most important questions are, what do you stand for fundamentally?''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Genrich declined repeated opportunities to explain what, precisely, the mayor of Green Bay could do about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his city.\par \par \uc2 Still, Republicans running for mayor find themselves doing a political tap dance, trying to de-emphasize but not disavow their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, which is not an electoral winner in Democratic cities. In Green Bay, Mr. Biden won 53 percent of the vote in 2020; last year, Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , a Democrat, took 55 percent of the city's vote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Genrich's opponent in Tuesday's officially nonpartisan election, Chad Weininger, is a former state legislator who cast a series of votes to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights before last year's Supreme Court ruling. Now, as television ads and campaign mail blast his stance and label him ''MAGA Chad'' to emphasize his Republican politics, he is trying to change the subject.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm running for mayor, I'm not debating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Mr. Weininger said. ''We could have discussions about nuclear arms, but guess what? Can't do anything about it. We can have discussions about securing our borders, but there's nothing we can do about it.''\par \par \uc2 National Democratic organizations that do not typically involve themselves in local elections are using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to promote and raise money for candidates who back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Emily's List, a group that backs women who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, has endorsed mayoral candidates in Jacksonville, Fla., Madison, Wis., and Lincoln, Neb.\par \par \uc2 In Lincoln, where Mr. Biden won 54 percent of the vote in 2020, Mayor \uc2 Leirion Gaylor Baird\uc2 , a Democrat, said her constituents had demanded to know what she could do about proposed legislation in the Nebraska Legislature that would restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Her answer: speak out against the bills.\par \par \uc2 Voters, Ms. Gaylor Baird said, are ''much more interested in knowing where people stand. So I expect that people will want to know where I stand on this issue, even if it isn't a local issue typically.''\par \par \uc2 Her main opponent, Suzanne Geist, a Republican state senator who has sponsored bills to restrict or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Nebraska, said her actions in the State Capitol should have little bearing on how she would run the state's capital city. She said she would prefer to focus on issues like public safety and the health of the city's business community.\par \par \uc2 Talking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ms. Geist said, is ''a way of avoiding what the present issues are and trying to get the public wrapped around something that really has nothing to do with the mayor's office or the mayor's race.''\par \par \uc2 Past opposition to some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has become a political liability even for candidates who support them now. In Chicago, \uc2 Paul Vallas\uc2 , the former Chicago Public Schools chief executive who is running for mayor, is being attacked by his more liberal opponent, Brandon Johnson, for a 2009 television interview in which Mr. Vallas said, ''Fundamentally, I oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vallas's statement, which he made when he being asked about possibly running for state office as a Republican, came after he had declared himself ''personally pro-choice'' but said he would favor banning some late-term abortions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Johnson is now broadcasting ads with a clip of Mr. Vallas's statement that he opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; Mr. Vallas has responded with advertising declaring that he supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In an interview on Sunday at a Greek restaurant, Mr. Vallas said Mr. Johnson had taken his past \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments out of context.\par \par \uc2 ''It's had some impact,'' he acknowledged.\par \par \uc2 In other races, municipal candidates are trying to find ways to make their cities have some influence over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Daniela Vel\u225\'00\'E1zquez, a public relations executive running for the St. Louis Board of Aldermen, is campaigning on continuing the city's policy of providing money for women seeking abortions to travel across the Mississippi River to Illinois, where the procedure remains legal. While \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became illegal in Missouri after the Supreme Court's decision, Ms. Vel\u225\'00\'E1zquez said many in St. Louis supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''I have been knocking on doors and people have looked at our lit and been like, 'Oh, you know, pro-choice,''' she said. ''Then they say, 'Yeah, I'm going to vote for you.'''\par \par \uc2 Democrats are open in their belief at the current moment, the best way to win votes is to focus on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fight.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the No. 1 issue in 2023,'' said \uc2 Ben Wikler\uc2 , the chairman of the Democratic Party of Wisconsin, which has given hundreds of thousands of dollars to back Mr. Genrich in Green Bay and Mayor Cory Mason in Racine, who is making similar arguments there. ''It's the No. 1 issue that moves voters that normally vote Republican to vote for someone else and it's the No. 1 issue to get Democrats off the couch and casting ballots.''\par \par \uc2 In November, Racine asked voters on the midterm-election ballot if Wisconsin's 1849 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban should be repealed -- and 71 percent said yes. Mr. Mason is now running television ads highlighting his stance in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and attacking his opponent.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Mason said, comes up in his discussions with voters as much as snow plowing, public safety and housing.\par \par \uc2 ''These two big issues around freedom, the freedom to vote and the freedom to make your own health care decisions, they are every bit as front and center in this race as anything else that we deal with at the municipal level,'' Mr. Mason said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mason's opponent, Henry Perez, a Republican city alderman opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said voters in Racine did not care much about the issue. He said that he did not remember how he had voted in the November \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendum, and that too much fuss was being made over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  being banned in Racine when it was available across the state line in Illinois, roughly 25 miles south of the city.\par \par \uc2 ''A lot of people I've talked to say, 'Henry, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , really?''' Mr. Perez said. ''What do we care about it here? I mean, it's not a thing that we do. And there's always options like going out of town, you know, or going over to the next state to take care of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they need to.''\par \par \uc2 Mitch Smith contributed reporting from Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Mitch Smith contributed reporting from Chicago.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA VEL\u193\'00\'C1ZQUEZ, who is running for the St. Louis Board of Aldermen, wants to continue the city's policy of helping women travel to Illinois for abortions. (PHOTOGRAPH BY WHITNEY CURTIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); CHAD WEININGER is facing ads blasting his votes against \uc2  rights as he runs for mayor in Green Bay, Wis.; ERIC GENRICH, Green Bay's mayor, repeatedly declined to explain what his office could do about \uc2  in his city. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY KAYLA WOLF FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); LEIRION GAYLOR BAIRD, the mayor of Lincoln, Neb., told constituents to speak out against anti-\uc2  legislation.; SUZANNE GEIST, Ms. Gaylor Baird's main opponent, has sponsored bills to restrict or ban \uc2  in Nebraska. 