\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Wyoming Becomes the First State to Outlaw Pills for Medical  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen and Pam Belluck \par \uc2 1,527 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers could serve six months in prison under the law, one of a growing number of efforts by conservative states to target the pills.\par \par \uc2 Wyoming on Friday became the first state to ban the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, adding momentum to a growing push by conservative states and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups to target medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the method now used in a majority of pregnancy terminations in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Wyoming's new law comes as a preliminary ruling is expected soon by a Texas judge that could order the \uc2 U.S. Food and Drug Administration\uc2  to withdraw its approval of mifepristone, the first pill in the two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen. Such a ruling, if it stands, could upend how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is provided nationally, affecting states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal as well as states with bans and restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Legislation to ban or add restrictions on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been introduced in several states this year, including a bill in Texas that would not only ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills but also require internet service providers to take steps to block medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  websites so people in Texas could not view them.\par \par \uc2 In these states, proposals to block or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have typically been introduced along with other anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, a reflection of the range of obstacles to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  these states have tried to erect since the Supreme Court overturned the national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last June.\par \par \uc2 Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already outlawed in states that have total bans, since those bans already prohibit all forms of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gov. Mark Gordon of Wyoming, a Republican, signed that state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill ban on the same day that he said he would allow another more sweeping measure banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to become law without his signature. That law, which takes effect on Sunday, would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under almost all circumstances, making it a felony to provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I have acted without bias and after extensive prayer, to allow these bills to become law,'' Mr. Gordon wrote in a letter to Wyoming's secretary of state released on Friday evening.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gordon said in the letter that he withheld his signature from the broader \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban because he feared it would complicate matters in an ongoing legal battle over an earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban passed by Wyoming legislators.\par \par \uc2 The broader ban outlaws medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well, and the measure that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills would mostly have the effect of adding additional penalties for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 Both laws are likely to be challenged quickly in court by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, who will seek to prevent the bans from taking effect while the legal challenge proceeds. A previously enacted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban has so far been blocked by the courts after providers and others filed suit claiming that the law violated the Wyoming state Constitution's guarantee of freedom in health care decisions. The newly enacted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is an attempt to circumvent that constitutional provision by declaring that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not health care.\par \par \uc2 Wyoming's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill law would take effect on July 1 and would make it illegal to ''prescribe, dispense, distribute, sell or use any drug for the purpose of procuring or performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' Doctors or anyone else found guilty of violating this law would be charged with a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in prison and a $9,000 fine. The law explicitly says that pregnant patients will be exempt from charges and penalties.\par \par \uc2 Wyoming has only one clinic that provides abortions, Women's Health & Family Care Clinic in Jackson. It provides only medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not the surgical procedure.\par \par \uc2 Earlier versions of the bill had named specific drugs: mifepristone and two brand-name versions of it as well as misoprostol, the second drug used in the medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen.\par \par \uc2 But doctors testified in objection, pointing out that misoprostol, in particular, had many other medical uses, including helping pregnant patients successfully give birth. The doctors raised concerns that pharmacists would be fearful of stocking any of the drugs, and some Republicans said names of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications could simply be changed to get around the law. As a result, the final language was broadened to outlaw using any medication for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without mentioning specific drugs.\par \par \uc2 At least three other bills have been introduced in 2023 that seek to ban medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Iowa, the bill did not make it to a vote before the legislative session ended, and in Hawaii, a Democratic state, the bill seems unlikely to succeed.\par \par \uc2 A bill introduced in Texas, a state that already bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , includes many provisions that seek to close off any access to pills, including making it difficult for Texas patients to learn about or use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services outside of the state. The bill would make it illegal to manufacture, distribute or ''provide an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drug in any manner to or from any person or location in this state.''\par \par \uc2 It would also make it illegal to ''create, edit, upload, publish, host, maintain, or register a domain name for an internet website, platform, or other interactive computer service that assists or facilitates a person's effort in obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drug.''\par \par \uc2 Many patients learn about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  options from websites like Plan C, a clearinghouse of information about medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And a growing number of patients in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans are arranging to receive pills through telemedicine websites like Aid Access, a European-based service that has pills shipped to any state from India, and Hey Jane, one of several American-based services that will provide pills to patients who travel to a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal and where they can receive the medication by mail in those states.\par \par \uc2 In addition to Wyoming and states with total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, 15 states have enacted restrictions on access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Those restrictions range from requiring that the drugs be provided by a physician to requiring the patient have an in-person visit with a doctor. Several states, including Texas and Arizona, have outlawed the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, and bills to ban mailing pills have been introduced in at least three other states this year.\par \par \uc2 ''We are seeing efforts to further bar access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents recognize that even with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in effect in 12 states and lack of access in an additional two, patients are still able to obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills,'' said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , state policy analyst for the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . ''Now, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have turned to the courts, attorneys general and state legislatures to further limit access to pills.''\par \par \uc2 Since January, when newly elected legislatures began to convene for the first time since the Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization decision ended the national right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more than 500 bills in states across the country have been proposed that are related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some states where Democratic legislatures have strong -- or even unexpected -- majorities are moving to strengthen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections. In Minnesota, the first bill of the 2023 legislature, which made it harder for future legislatures and governors to water down those protections, was signed in January by Gov. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 , a Democrat. In Michigan, the legislature repealed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, is expected to sign it.\par \par \uc2 But a majority of new bills aim to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. And with several states now wrapping up their legislative sessions, bills are starting to land on governors' desks.\par \par \uc2 Under the other new Wyoming law, the ''Life Is a Human Right Act,'' performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or administering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication would be considered a felony, punishable by up to five years in prison, and doctors would have their licenses revoked. The law bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with narrow exceptions for rape, incest and dire risks to the pregnant patient's life or health.\par \par \uc2 ''While other states are pushing an extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda, comparable to North Korea's and China's inhumane laws, Wyoming is a pro-life state, affirming that life is a human right and ensuring that women have real support,'' said state Representative Rachel Rodriguez-Williams, the bill's sponsor.\par \par \uc2 The law is intended to replace an existing ban, which is now on hold because of a legal challenge over its constitutionality. How that affects the actions of the Wyoming Supreme Court, though, remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 At issue is the definition of health care: Under the Wyoming Constitution, residents have the right to make their own health care decisions. So the new law stipulates that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not health care.\par \par \uc2 ''Instead of being health care, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the intentional termination of the life of an unborn baby,'' the new law states. ''It is within the authority of the state of Wyoming to determine reasonable and necessary restrictions upon \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including its prohibition.''\par \par \uc2 Several states have recently introduced bills to ban or restrict drugs like mifepristone, the first pill in a two-drug regimen. (PHOTOGRAPH BY CAITLIN OCHS/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230318ej3i0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
