\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In Iowa, Harris Says Attack on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill Threatens Health Care\b0\par\par\uc2 By Zolan Kanno-Youngs \par \uc2 883 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ''If politicians start using the court to undo doctors' decisions, imagine where that can lead,'' Ms. Harris said at a discussion on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with local lawmakers and medical officials.\par \par \uc2 DES MOINES -- Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said on Thursday that a lawsuit seeking to overturn federal approval of a widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill amounted to an attack on ''our public health system as a whole.''\par \par \uc2 During her first trip to Iowa as vice president, Ms. Harris portrayed Republican attempts to impose a nationwide ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as immoral and extreme.\par \par \uc2 ''If politicians start using the court to undo doctors' decisions, imagine where that can lead,'' Ms. Harris said as a judge in Texas considered whether he would issue a preliminary injunction that could take the pill, mifepristone, off the market.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has taken a lead role on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as President Biden prepares to announce an expected run for re-election. Without the votes in Congress to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections into law, the White House hopes Ms. Harris can help sustain the sort of anger that motivated Democratic voters during the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 In her appearance Thursday at Grand View University, Ms. Harris framed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue as part of a broader struggle for health care and privacy, a strategy aimed at galvanizing the broadest coalition of voters.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not only about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' Ms. Harris said, adding that overturning F.D.A. approval for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication could set a dangerous precedent, potentially affecting the availability of other medications.\par \par \uc2 The last-minute trip to Iowa, planned by the vice president's team only in the past few days, is part of a push by Ms. Harris to get out into the country more to overcome an impression from allies and critics alike that she has not forged a definitive role in the administration.\par \par \uc2 Top Republicans have flocked to Iowa in recent weeks in anticipation of the 2024 Iowa caucuses, including former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 ; former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ; Nikki Haley, the former United Nations ambassador; Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida and Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have overhauled their primary calendar, replacing Iowa with South Carolina as the party's first nominating contest. But the rush of Republicans to Iowa presented an opportunity for Ms. Harris to call attention to restrictions that could be imposed by Republican-led legislatures.\par \par \uc2 ''We need to show the difference that while Republicans are taking health care rights away from them, we in the Democratic Party are saying that is not acceptable,'' said Elizabeth Naftali, a deputy finance chair of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Naftali said that Democrats could not allow a ''steamroll by Republicans'' just because the primary calendar had changed.\par \par \uc2 Most abortions are now banned in more than a dozen states following the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade last year. While Iowa has not banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is one of many states the administration fears could soon enact more severe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the Iowa Supreme Court found that there was no right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the state's constitution. A ban on the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy has been blocked by a state judge since 2019 but Gov. \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 , a Republican, has appealed the decision to the higher court. The state currently bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Most Iowans -- 61 percent -- believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, according to a Des Moines Register/\uc2 Mediacom\uc2  poll last fall. Thirty-three percent say it should be illegal in most or all cases, and 6 percent are not sure.\par \par \uc2 ''We have just seen a lot of panic and fear among patients who are worried,'' said Mazie Stilwell, the director of public affairs for Planned Parenthood Advocates of Iowa.\par \par \uc2 White House officials acknowledged that there was only so much that they could do to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access without Congress, but many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates are calling for policies that would protect both medical officials providing abortions and those seeking them.\par \par \uc2 ''What I know feels frustrating for me and many organizers on the ground is we keep having meetings but there's not any action,'' said Renee Bracey Sherman, the founder and executive director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy group We Testify.\par \par \uc2 No major policy announcements came on Thursday. But Ms. Harris described those pushing for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as ''extremist so-called leaders who purport and profess to hail themselves as a beacon of freedom and opportunity.''\par \par \uc2 Stefanie Brown James, a co-founder of the Collective PAC, an organization dedicated to electing African American officials, said such blunt messaging would be imperative for both Ms. Harris and Mr. Biden in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 ''In the event \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  continues to be his second in command, it's important for her now to be out having conversations as much as it is for him to be,'' Ms. James said. ''This issue is not going away anytime soon.''\par \par \uc2 Vice President Kamala Harris in Philadelphia on Feb. 3. In Iowa on Thursday, she said efforts to ban \uc2  nationwide were immoral. (PHOTOGRAPH BY AL DRAGO FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A15. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230317ej3h0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
