\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 North Dakota Supreme Court Blocks  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban, Saying Constitution Protects It\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani and David W. Chen \par \uc2 929 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The court's opinion is not the final word on the case, but it upheld a lower court's decision to suspend the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 North Dakota's Supreme Court upheld a lower court's decision to block a ban on abortions in the state, and said the state Constitution protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in some situations.\par \par \uc2 The ruling means \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state remains legal until nearly 22 weeks after a women's last period, while the case proceeds in a lower court.\par \par \uc2 Though the state Supreme Court's decision is not the final word on the matter, it is notable for its analysis of the state Constitution. The court went beyond the narrow question it was asked: whether the lower court judge had overstepped his power in blocking the ban.\par \par \uc2 In a majority opinion, the ruling said that judge was within his rights but added that the state Constitution protects ''the right to enjoy and defend life and a right to pursue and obtain safety,'' which includes the right of a pregnant woman to ''obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to preserve her life or her health.''\par \par \uc2 State constitutions have become key arbiters in the nation's state-by-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  battles. The overturning of Roe v. Wade by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  last year kicked the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states to decide. Since then, lawsuits have been filed in about a dozen states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights lawyers arguing that the prohibitions are unconstitutional under state guarantees of privacy, health, liberty or family planning.\par \par \uc2 So far, two state Supreme Courts have made final decisions, and they were split: South Carolina ruled \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was included in its constitutional protections; Idaho ruled its Constitution did not protect the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In North Dakota, the state law prohibiting nearly all abortions was blocked three times last year by Judge Bruce A. Romanick of the Burleigh County District Court, after North Dakota's sole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, Red River Women's Clinic, filed a lawsuit against the ban last June.\par \par \uc2 Judge Romanick first issued a temporary restraining order against the ban a day before it was set to take effect in July, then extended the ban in August and October, saying the North Dakota Constitution protected the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a relatively rare legal maneuver, the North Dakota attorney general, Drew Wrigley, filed a motion asking the high court to determine if Judge Romanick had abused his discretion by blocking the ban from taking effect while the court case proceeds.\par \par \uc2 Four of the five justices on the state Supreme Court were appointed by Gov. \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , a Republican, and one was elected in a nonpartisan election.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wrigley, who supports the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, said the state's high court ''appears to have taken on the role of a legislative body, a role our Constitution does not afford them.''\par \par \uc2 The court also said the state legislature had the authority to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates in the case seeking to overturn the ban ''demonstrated likely success on the merits that there is a fundamental right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the limited instances of life-saving and health-preserving circumstances, and the statute is not narrowly tailored to satisfy strict scrutiny.''\par \par \uc2 Legislators and policymakers in North Dakota have been anticipating the Supreme Court's decision ever since oral arguments were presented in November.\par \par \uc2 One bill under consideration that was intended to go into effect if the Supreme Court upheld the lower court's injunction was introduced in January by the State Senator Janne Myrdal, a Republican. The bill would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , except when a pregnant woman's life is in danger. The bill would also allow for exceptions in cases of rape or incest up until six weeks of pregnancy, before most people know they are pregnant. Under the bill, doctors who perform abortions which violate the law could face misdemeanor or felony charges.\par \par \uc2 The Senate overwhelmingly passed the bill in January. A key House committee has also signed off on the bill, which now awaits a vote by the full House.\par \par \uc2 The North Dakota attorney general's office commended the legislature for advancing the new bill restricting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying lawmakers ''will now have the opportunity to enact the will of North Dakotans.''\par \par \uc2 Red River Women's Clinic stopped offering abortions in the state, instead moving a short drive across the border to Moorhead, Minn., in August.\par \par \uc2 But attorneys representing the clinic say it is important to ensure that the ban does not take effect, so that pregnant patients facing medical emergencies can receive abortions in hospitals and from their doctors.\par \par \uc2 ''This law forces physicians to delay urgent medical care to patients and forces patients to endure these unnecessary medical risks,'' said \uc2 Genevieve Scott\uc2 , senior counsel at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Scott said the state's law offers extremely narrow exceptions for medical emergencies. Under the current law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed if the pregnant woman's life is threatened, but a doctor cannot perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent serious injury. Many other states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently prohibited offer exceptions for cases where there is a threat to a patient's ''major bodily function.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Scott said the ban would have prevented physicians from providing care in cases of miscarriage, ectopic pregnancy and complications where there was no chance of a fetus surviving.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230317ej3h0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
