\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Polish Activist Convicted Of Giving  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Monika Pronczuk \par \uc2 1,107 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It was the first conviction of its type in the country and in Europe, offering a glimpse of the implications of a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A court in Poland on Tuesday found a women's rights activist guilty of aiding an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by providing pills, the first conviction of its type in the country and Europe, highlighting the growing risks for women in a nation with the strictest anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law on the continent.\par \par \uc2 The activist, Justyna Wydrzynska, a founder of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dream Team, an organization that provides information about how to safely terminate pregnancies, was sentenced to eight months of community service, but vowed to continue her work.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't feel guilty at all,'' Ms. Wydrzynska said on Tuesday, surrounded by scores of people who had stood outside the court building in Warsaw for hours in the rain to show their support. ''I don't accept this verdict. I will continue picking up the phone calls.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wydrzynska said she would appeal the verdict. The woman she helped never used the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills and had a miscarriage, according to Ms. Wydrzynska.\par \par \uc2 The prosecutor had asked for 10 months of community service, saying that ''her behavior shows, even manifests, that she would do it again without hesitation.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wydrzynska had acted, the prosecutor said, ''with direct intent,'' adding, ''The degree of the social harmfulness should be assessed as significant, taking into account the violated good -- life in the prenatal period.''\par \par \uc2 The case in Poland offered a glimpse of the implications of a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, and activists in the United States were closely watching the verdict. In the 13 American states that have outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in 2022, supplying medication to end pregnancies is governed by a patchwork of laws.\par \par \uc2 Last week in Texas, which has banned abortions at all stages of pregnancy except in life-threatening medical emergencies, a man filed a lawsuit against three women who he said had helped his ex-wife obtain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 In Poland, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been legal for decades under Communist rule, performing the procedure was outlawed in 1993 under pressure from the Roman Catholic Church, leaving exceptions for fetal abnormalities, rape, incest and when a woman's health or life is at risk.\par \par \uc2 Despite the exceptions, in practice, the law constitutes a near total ban because rape victims must obtain a special certificate from a prosecutor and many doctors fear breaching the law because of what many view as its ambiguous phrasing.\par \par \uc2 And though a woman cannot be prosecuted for getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills under the law, helping someone to terminate a pregnancy is illegal. This means that the provider, not the woman, faces legal consequences, with a potential penalty of up to three years in prison.\par \par \uc2 The law was tightened further in 2021, scrapping the exception for fetal abnormalities, despite the largest protests in Poland's post-Communist history. Since the ban was tightened, at least three women have died in Poland after doctors refused an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or delayed the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The legislation had repeatedly failed to pass in the Polish Parliament but, in a trajectory that resembles the overturning of Roe v. Wade, Poland's top court said that abortions for fetal abnormalities were unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 In the ruling, the court's president, \uc2 Julia Przylebska\uc2 , said that allowing abortions in cases of fetal abnormality amounted to ''eugenic practices with regard to an unborn child, thus denying it the respect and protection of human dignity.''\par \par \uc2 Because the Polish Constitution guarantees a right to life, she added, terminating a pregnancy based on the health of the fetus amounted to ''a directly forbidden form of discrimination.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wydrzynska, who has been an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activist in Poland for the past 16 years, said in an interview last summer with The Times that she had always been careful to provide only instructions on buying and using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications, and not to provide the pills themselves.\par \par \uc2 But in February 2020, she said in the interview, she received a desperate message from a woman identified as Anna who was seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The call from the woman, who, the court heard, was in an abusive relationship, revived Ms. Wydrzynska's own traumatic memories of a violent relationship and getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It prompted her, she said, to do something she had never done before -- send the woman a package of pills.\par \par \uc2 ''I sent Anna pills because I found out that she had experienced violence like me,'' Ms. Wydrzynska told the court in her closing statement on Tuesday, barely holding back tears.\par \par \uc2 The woman's partner read the messages between the two women, the court heard, and reported Ms. Wydrzynska to the police. She was charged with ''possession of drugs without authorization in order to place them on the market'' and ''aiding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' The court in Warsaw found Ms. Wydrzynska guilty of aiding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by sending misoprostol pills, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication, and sentenced her to 30 hours of community service a month for eight months.\par \par \uc2 Ordo Iuris, a Polish Catholic legal organization and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that was registered as a civil party in the trial, had demanded prison time for Ms. Wydrzynska but had no legal right to do so.\par \par \uc2 A representative of the group, Magdalena Majkowska, told the court on Tuesday that Ms. Wydrzynska's conviction should ''be regarded as a significant step towards real respect for the right to life of unborn children in Poland.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wydrzynska said the court's justification for its decision had not been made public.\par \par \uc2 ''I am innocent,'' she said. ''I say it loudly -- the state is to blame. It has failed me, Anna, Iza from Pszczyna, Agnieszka from Cz\u196\'00\'C4\u8482\'21\'22stochowa and millions of women in this country,'' she added, referring to the women who died after having been refused abortions.\par \par \uc2 Rights activists said that Ms. Wydrzynska's case should never have been brought to court.\par \par \uc2 Keina Yoshida, a senior legal adviser of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has offices in New York and other cities around the globe, said: ''Her prosecution sets a dangerous precedent for the targeting of human rights defenders in Poland who are working to advance \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and challenge Poland's de facto ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 ''Criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prosecuting those providing assistance and support to people in need of health care is wrong. It contravenes international human rights treaties and flies in the face of modern medical practice and \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  guidelines.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A8. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230315ej3f0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
