\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Foes Want to Make Women Afraid to Get Help From Friends\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 979 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 18\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The month after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, a mother of two in Texas who had filed for divorce from her husband discovered she was pregnant. Determined not to have another child and worried that her husband would try to use the pregnancy to make her stay with him, she did what many of us would do and turned to two friends for help.\par \par \uc2 In text messages that are now part of a chilling lawsuit, her friends responded with warmth and solidarity. One told her about Aid Access, an organization based in Vienna that ships \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to people in places where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned. Then the same friend texted that she had found someone nearby who could supply the medication. She and another friend both offered to let the woman go through the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their homes. ''Mistakes happen,'' the second friend texted. ''You can't spiral. Hopefully this is the slap in the body that you need to remove yourself from him.''\par \par \uc2 Now the ex-husband, Marcus Silva, is getting his revenge. Last week, he filed a wrongful-death lawsuit against his ex-wife's two friends and the woman who allegedly provided the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills his ex-wife took, seeking a million dollars from each of them. (Because the suit seems likely to send abuse their way, I'm not including the women's names.)\par \par \uc2 Silva's case appears to have the backing of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, since he's being represented by Jonathan F. Mitchell, the former Texas solicitor general who devised Texas' \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty law, which gives private citizens the power to sue others for ''conduct that aids or abets the performance or inducement of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' His legal team also includes \uc2 Briscoe Cain\uc2 , a prominent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponent in the Texas House, and three members of the Thomas More Society, a right-wing Catholic legal organization. ''Assisting a self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas,'' says the lawsuit, is ''an act of murder.''\par \par \uc2 This case has several harrowing implications. First, it makes particularly vivid the way \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibitions give men control over women. In the text messages reproduced in the lawsuit, Silva's ex-wife wrote, of her pregnancy, that she knew Silva would ''use it against me'' and ''try to act like he has some right to the decision.'' Given that he is now suing her friends, she seems to have understood him well. What she might not have understood is how much political power he'd be able to muster on behalf of his patriarchal prerogatives.\par \par \uc2 According to Melissa Murray, a law professor at \uc2 New York University\uc2 , it's significant that the lawsuit wasn't filed under Mitchell's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty law. Instead, it's a wrongful-death case, which Murray sees as a bid to win judicial recognition of fetal personhood in Texas law.\par \par \uc2 ''Texas may prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but not on the grounds that it is a species of homicide -- that is, the killing of a person,'' she said. Texas lawmakers have, in the past, introduced legislation classifying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as homicide, which would make either having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or performing one punishable by the death penalty, but the bills have never succeeded. If the idea of fetal personhood is normalized in the law through wrongful-death cases like Silva's, applying murder statutes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes easier to imagine. ''Jonathan Mitchell is playing five-dimensional chess with this,'' Murray said.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to say whether the lawsuit has a chance, since that will depend on which judge hears it. Joanna Grossman, a visiting law professor at Stanford, found the filing absurd, saying, ''It's not written in a way to convince anybody about a serious legal argument.'' But far-right judges don't necessarily need serious legal arguments. After all, in another Texas lawsuit, we're waiting to see whether a federal judge appointed by Donald Trump takes the unprecedented step of revoking F.D.A. approval of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug mifepristone. There are lots of judges, said Grossman, ''just doing the bidding of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.''\par \par \uc2 Whatever happens legally, the message to women in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans is unmistakable: You're on your own. ''No one is going to follow whether this ex-husband collects damages for this \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Grossman. They're just going to see the news that the women accused of helping Silva's ex-wife were publicly humiliated and put in grave financial jeopardy, and they're going to think twice about confiding in their friends about an unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''Mitchell's trying to get at the whole information network, so that people really, truly are isolated emotionally and can't trust anybody,'' said Grossman.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit includes a photograph of Silva's ex-wife and her friends dressed up like characters from ''The Handmaid's Tale'' for Halloween last year. It's presented as evidence that they ''celebrated the murder,'' but I suspect it was included to make them identifiable and punish them for their cheekiness.\par \par \uc2 It turns out the women's in-joke was more on the nose than they could have realized. In the novel, women aren't allowed to communicate with one another except in pious stock phrases, and it takes months for the protagonist to realize that her seemingly meek shopping partner, Ofglen, is a rebel. ''Don't say a word,'' Ofglen warns. ''In any way.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230314ej3e00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
