\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  Under Fire for Not Offering  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill in 21 States\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck and Julie Creswell \par \uc2 1,403 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The chain's situation shows the complicated legal and reputational landscape that it and its competitors must navigate in the post-Roe era.\par \par \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  landed at the center of a consumer and political firestorm in recent days, after saying it would not dispense an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill in 21 states where Republican attorneys general have threatened legal action against pharmacies that try to distribute the medication.\par \par \uc2 The company confirmed late last week that it would not distribute mifepristone, the first pill in a two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, weeks after it and other large pharmacy chains received letters signed by those attorneys general. In each of the states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is either banned or laws or proposed or pending legislation would prevent pharmacists from dispensing pills.\par \par \uc2 The situation starkly reflects the patchwork legal framework that sizable pharmacy chains like \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , which is the second largest in the country, must navigate after last summer's Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade, which for nearly 50 years had enshrined a national right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Since then, 13 states have made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal while others have tightened access to it, including through restrictions related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 While chains like CVS and \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  face the same legal and reputational quagmire as \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , they are staying quiet on the matter.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , Fraser Engerman, said Tuesday that the chain had always said it would dispense mifepristone where state laws allowed pharmacists to do so and that it had decided before it received the letters from the attorneys general that it would face legal repercussions in those 21 states. The Food and Drug Administration said in January that retail pharmacies could become certified to offer mifepristone, which could previously be dispensed only by clinics, doctors and a few mail-order pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 ''We understood the moment we decided to participate in the F.D.A. program that we would have to navigate these two set of restrictions, including complete \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and requirements around who can actually dispense the medication,'' Mr. Engerman said.\par \par \uc2 ''We have said from the beginning we will dispense this medication wherever we legally can once we are certified by the F.D.A.,'' he added, ''but at the same time we have to follow the law.''\par \par \uc2 But by publicly confirming that it would not dispense in the 21 states, \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  faced blowback from consumers and politicians alike.\par \par \uc2 There were social media calls for shoppers to boycott the company. Gov. \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois, where \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  has its headquarters, held a video call on Friday with the company's senior leadership, including the chief executive, \uc2 Rosalind Brewer\uc2 . Mr. Pritzker, a Democrat, urged them to rethink their stance in response to the Republican threats.\par \par \uc2 Another Democrat, Gov. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  of California, said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that his state ''won't be doing business'' with \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  ''or any company that cowers to the extremists and puts women's lives at risk.'' Brandon Richards, Mr. Newsom's deputy communications director, later said the governor's office would scrutinize state contracts and survey agencies dealing with health care, adding that it was ''reviewing all relationships between \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and the state.''\par \par \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 ' stock was down almost 4 percent, to $34.14, by the close of trading on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Pharmacists Association\uc2  had urged the F.D.A. to allow retail pharmacies to distribute mifepristone, even though the medication is unlikely to generate significant revenue. In a statement at the time, the association said it wanted the agency ''to level the playing field by permitting any pharmacy that chooses to dispense this product to become certified.''\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the policy change was announced, \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS said they planned to become certified and offer mifepristone in states where laws would allow pharmacies to dispense it. The pharmacies did not say they would dispense the pill in all states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 Their statements indicated that states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans would be off limits and that they would not try to dispense the pills in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained legal but other legislation could prevent pharmacies from providing the drug -- by requiring, for example, that only doctors give it to patients.\par \par \uc2 In Alaska, Iowa and Montana -- whose attorneys general signed the letter sent to the pharmacy chains -- \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still legal, but laws or proposed restrictions apply to the provision of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. The same is true of Kansas, whose attorney general sent a separate letter to the chains and where a law requiring physicians to dispense the drug is currently enjoined by a legal challenge.\par \par \uc2 ''Violating the has-to-be-done-by-a-physician requirements in some of these states is punishable by jail,'' Mr. Engerman said. ''In other states, it's punishable by a civil fine, and in a number of them it's punishable by licensing sanctions. And so these are restrictions that present real risks to pharmacists.''\par \par \uc2 The stakes are high for \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , CVS, \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  and the other companies with large pharmaceutical arms, including \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2 . The Republican attorneys general wield powerful weapons, including the ability to press charges against companies or individual pharmacists who dispense the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills or even pull the company's pharmaceutical license in the state.\par \par \uc2 In an emailed statement, \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  said it was continuing to monitor and evaluate the situation. CVS, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Costco\uc2 , \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  did not respond to emails seeking comment.\par \par \uc2 ''All of the pharmacies are facing the same problem,'' said Andrew Gilman, the chief executive of CommCore Consulting Group, a crisis communications firm. ''But \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , as the first one to be publicly identified with going along with the state attorneys general request, will face the biggest hit to its reputation.''\par \par \uc2 The fact that the major pharmacy chains have said since January that they would abide by all relevant state laws has gotten subsumed in the swirl of heightened tensions around the availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. And with medication accounting for more than half of abortions in the United States, the pills have increasingly become the focus of lawsuits, legislation and other tactics.\par \par \uc2 ''There is a lot of noise and confusion out there about what's going on with respect to the availability of these drugs,'' said Ilisa Bernstein, the interim chief executive of the \uc2 American Pharmacists Association\uc2 . She added, ''The court cases, the letters, that's just creating even more complication and confusion, particularly for pharmacies.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Bernstein said pharmacies had a range of concerns to worry about.\par \par \uc2 ''It's the states that hold a pharmacy's license, so they're going to follow state law so that they can maintain their license,'' she said, adding: ''It's the safety of pharmacy staff, too. Just having the ability for your staff to come into work safely and then work in a safe space.''\par \par \uc2 Currently, few pharmacies have completed the certification process to be able to distribute mifepristone. The process is overseen by the two companies that manufacture the pill and involves logistical measures that go beyond the steps pharmacies use with most other medications, such as designating an employee to ensure compliance. It also requires that pharmacies keep confidential the names of the certified health providers who prescribe mifepristone to protect their privacy and safety.\par \par \uc2 A chain like \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  would not be able to list a doctor's name in a companywide database, for example. It would have to keep that information restricted to the store that filled the doctor's prescriptions.\par \par \uc2 ''The manufacturers are being very cautious in how they release the product, they're being very cautious in terms of certifying pharmacies to be able to dispense the product, and even others in the supply chain, such as wholesalers, are being very cautious to make sure that they're selling it to pharmacies who can legally dispense it,'' Ms. Bernstein said.\par \par \uc2 Pharmacies like \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  and CVS already dispense the second medication in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen, misoprostol, which has never been as tightly restricted as mifepristone. It is used for several different medical conditions and is available through a typical prescription process.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Robbins and Shawn Hubler contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Robbins and Shawn Hubler contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 The stakes are high for Walgreens and other pharmacy companies. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MICHAEL DWYER/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page B1, B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230308ej3800053\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
