\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Florida Aims To Prohibit Abortions At Six Weeks\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen and Patricia Mazzei \par \uc2 631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The bills would tighten the current 15-week limit and offer narrow exceptions. Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  said he welcomed ''pro-life legislation.''\par \par \uc2 Florida Republicans introduced parallel bills on Tuesday in the House and Senate that would further restrict the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to six from 15 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 If a ban passes and is signed into law by Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Florida would prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before many women even realize they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Filed on the first day of the 2023 legislative session, the bills had been telegraphed for weeks, as Republican leaders who control Tallahassee sought to build on the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  momentum generated by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's overturning of Roe v. Wade last summer.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis, a Republican who is widely expected to run for president, said on Tuesday that he supported additional restrictions, though the bills also offer a reminder of the political complexities that he must navigate.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions appeal to Republicans, but 64 percent of Florida residents believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most or all cases, according to a recent poll by the Public Religion Research Institute. Legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also supported by the wider American public, whom Mr. DeSantis would have to face in a general election.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis signed a law last year banning abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, down from 24 weeks, without exceptions for rape or incest. That law was not as strict as what many conservatives have demanded. At least 13 states have bans that prohibit most abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are suing the state over the 15-week ban, though it remains in effect while the court case proceeds.\par \par \uc2 One of the first questions Mr. DeSantis fielded at a news conference on Tuesday, after his annual state of the state address, focused on the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills, which were filed shortly before his speech. The bills allow for exceptions to the proposed bans in cases of rape or incest until 15 weeks of pregnancy, or if the mother's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 ''I think those exceptions are sensible,'' Mr. DeSantis said. ''We welcome pro-life legislation.''\par \par \uc2 Kathleen Passidomo, a Naples Republican and the Senate president, said she supported the six-week ban. She had previously signaled that a 12-week ban might be more palatable to lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 ''My No. 1 priority was the inclusion, in whatever legislation we carry, was an exception for rape and incest,'' she told reporters in the State Capitol on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Paul Renner, a Palm Coast Republican and the House speaker, noted that the bill included ''trigger language,'' meaning that it would not take effect until after the Florida Supreme Court rules on the 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that legislators passed last year.\par \par \uc2 Among other provisions, the new bills would ban telemedicine for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and require that medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be dispensed in person by a doctor. Doctors who break the law would be punishable by up to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Democrats were quick to criticize both the contents of the legislation and the messengers.\par \par \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , the White House press secretary, told The Miami Herald: ''Politicians like Governor DeSantis espouse 'freedom for all,' while directly attacking the freedom to make one's own health care decisions.''\par \par \uc2 In an email, Representative Fentrice Driskell, a Tampa Democrat and the House minority leader, said the bills would essentially make ''all abortions impossible'' and adversely affect ''people from Louisiana, Mississippi, Alabama and other states in the South who otherwise would have looked to Florida for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''Politicians should never be involved in the medical decisions between a person and their doctor.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230308ej380003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
